WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:11.180
- So I'm really delighted
to introduce Liana Chua

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.830
who is a reader in anthropology

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.888
at Brunel University in London.

00:00:14.888 --> 00:00:17.960
She received her PhD
in social anthropology

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.170
from the University of Cambridge

00:00:20.170 --> 00:00:23.423
where she also held a research
fellowship in anthropology.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.930
She has worked with
rural Bidayuh communities

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:30.950
in Sarawak, Malaysian Borneo since 2003

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.857
looking first at Christian
conversion and ethnic politics.

00:00:34.857 --> 00:00:37.970
And more recently at
displacement environmental change

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.580
and world-making in the context

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:43.220
of dam construction and
resettlement schemes.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.840
Now she's turning her research trajectory

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.840
towards a more than human
engagements in Borneo

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.450
and focusing on some
of the ethical dilemmas

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.063
associated with orangutan conservation.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.600
She's widely published and has
a lot of wonderful articles

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.500
and books that I
recommend you all look at.

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:03.587
Her book is called

00:01:03.587 --> 00:01:05.730
"The Christianity of Culture:

00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:08.360
Conversion, Ethnic Citizenship,

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.380
and the Matter of Religion
in Malaysian Borneo"

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:12.923
which was published in 2012.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.290
She's also the co-editor

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.260
of a volume called

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:20.720
Distributed Objects: Meaning
and Mattering after Alfred Gell

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:23.150
which is published in 2013

00:01:23.150 --> 00:01:25.150
and her articles are
really quite wonderful.

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.920
I'll just mention a few.
There's a lot of them.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:30.310
There's one called the
Troubled landscapes,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.870
troubling anthropology:
co‐presence, necessity,

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.120
and the making of ethnographic knowledge

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.120
published in Journal of the
Royal Anthropological Institute

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.930
in 2015.

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.610
There's an American
ethnologist piece from 2012

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:48.420
called conversion continuity
and moral dilemmas

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.950
among Christian Bidayuhs
in Malaysian Borneo

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:55.630
and also a wonderful piece
called To know or not to know?

00:01:55.630 --> 00:01:58.350
Practices of knowledge and ignorance

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.950
among Bidayuhs in an
'impurely' Christian world

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.250
also with Journal of the Royal
Anthropological Institute

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:10.730
Today's session will be
kind of a special experiment

00:02:10.730 --> 00:02:13.500
in academic presentations.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.570
I've asked Liana if
she's willing to engage

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.330
in a kind of talk show style format.

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:22.210
The goal is to introduce
her work to all of you

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:24.930
and to allow her to
chart some of the themes

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:25.890
that have been going through

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:27.610
these many interesting connections

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:31.860
and to introduce the
importance of Malaysian Borneo

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.080
to a wide interdisciplinary
audience of Southeast Asianist

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.920
and also people interested
in anthropology.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:42.670
So the way this is gonna work
is we'll have a conversation

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.850
for about 20 to 30 minutes

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:48.879
and then after we do that, I'm
gonna open it up to questions

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:50.770
from the whole audience.

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.970
And I've asked Liana to
kind of speak broadly

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:56.150
in a way that can bring
people into her work.

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:58.100
So even if you haven't read her work yet

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:00.833
you'll get to learn a little
about it along the way.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:06.810
So with that said, let's
turn to the conversation.

00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:09.240
So firstly, thank you,
Liana for joining us.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:12.053
And that was really
delightful to have you here.

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:16.300
- Hi, it's nice to meet
everyone and thank you very much

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:19.630
for such a lovely, a warm and
generous introduction Erik.

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:21.270
It's really nice to meet you.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.700
- Yeah. So it's great to have you.

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:28.110
I wanna ask you about the
places you work in Borneo

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.280
and some of the people
that you've come to know

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.150
in your field work.

00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:34.780
So if you can just kind
of speak in an open sense

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:37.820
about where do you work
and who do you work with

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.770
and what are some of the major
issues confronting people

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:44.190
in the region and what are
some of the core themes

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:47.200
that you see animating your
work and how they've emerged

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:50.500
from your long-term research
in Malaysian Borneo?

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:51.333
- Sure. Okay.

00:03:51.333 --> 00:03:52.166
Thank you.

00:03:52.166 --> 00:03:53.800
Yeah, so I guess I sort of see myself

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.770
as an anthropologist of Borneo
and the kind of broader sense

00:03:57.770 --> 00:04:00.820
in the sense that I try to
approach Borneo as an Island

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:04.100
that's very much a sort
of interconnected space

00:04:04.100 --> 00:04:06.780
with many socio-cultural, linguistic,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.530
religious, political formations

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:12.210
but also environmental events
and processes and changes

00:04:12.210 --> 00:04:16.390
that transcend contemporary
national boundaries.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:17.860
So in that sense, I'm very interested

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:19.940
in the sorts of flows and connections,

00:04:19.940 --> 00:04:22.320
but also kind of gaps
in tensions that go on

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.190
within this broad Island.

00:04:24.190 --> 00:04:27.990
But having said that the bulk
of my research since 2003

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.990
has been with Bidayuhs as Eric mentioned.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.350
So Bidayuhs are small indigenous group.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.620
They live in the East Malaysian
State of Sarawak in Borneo

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:42.270
and they live in the sort
of mountainous hinterlands

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:43.740
of the State Capital, Kuching.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:48.440
And so for my PhD, I
lived for about 15 months

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:51.300
which is fairly standard for
anthropological field work

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.460
in a fairly large Bidayuh village.

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.660
And this village was a really
interesting place to be in

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:58.550
because it was in the
middle of this transition

00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:03.110
from a kind of
rice-planting adamistic past

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.180
to a very self-consciously
modern urbanized

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.890
Christian present and future.

00:05:08.890 --> 00:05:11.100
And I kind of turned
up towards the tail end

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.223
of that process.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:14.820
And one of the most interesting things

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.930
about doing research at that time

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:18.930
was that although the
vast majority of people

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:20.160
had converted to Christianity,

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.600
various denominations of
Christianity by that stage,

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.270
many of them still
remembered what it was like

00:05:25.270 --> 00:05:26.940
to do the old rituals.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.970
They had sort of firsthand experience

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.370
of what it was like to
convert to Christianity

00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:34.630
relatively recently kind of
in the last 10, 20 years.

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:35.463
And so there was that.

00:05:35.463 --> 00:05:37.600
There was this kind of
really rich living archive

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.670
of the conversion experience

00:05:39.670 --> 00:05:42.500
but there were also about 10
elderly animus practitioners

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.800
who are still carrying on and
still doing the odd rituals

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:46.490
and actually being helped in various ways

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:48.270
by their sort of younger
Christian Kayan neighbors.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.270
So it was a fascinating time to be there

00:05:50.270 --> 00:05:53.070
and so that was what eventually
gave rise to my book.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:55.950
And some of my earlier
publications on Christianity

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:57.897
where I kind of looked
at Bidayuh's experiences

00:05:57.897 --> 00:05:59.450
of converting to Christianity

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:02.480
got kind of enmeshed
with their experiences

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:05.470
of contemporary ethnic
religious politics in Malaysia,

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:07.270
which as many of you know is dominated

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:09.830
by kind of Malaysia Muslim majority,

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:11.220
but also how this was impacting

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:13.100
on their own internal
relations within the village

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.810
and the ways they were
negotiating their relationships

00:06:15.810 --> 00:06:19.010
with both the old animus
practitioners and the old spirits

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:21.270
were still very much
around and very present

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:22.210
in their lives.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:23.410
And so I've written about that

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.080
and maybe you can talk
about that bit later on.

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:27.810
So that was my PhD.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:31.020
After my PhD from about in 2007

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:34.210
I began doing what turned into
a quite long-term project.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:35.800
I'm still doing it.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.490
That traces the experiences of four small

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:40.620
and relatively remote Bidayuh villages

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.290
that got caught up in a dam construction

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:43.793
and resettlement project.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.840
So I basically trace the
experiences of these communities

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.463
all the way from the sort of preparations,

00:06:51.510 --> 00:06:53.130
when they were thinking about compensation

00:06:53.130 --> 00:06:54.100
who was going to go where,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.550
who was gonna move to
the resettlement site

00:06:56.550 --> 00:06:59.170
to a very sort of fractures

00:06:59.170 --> 00:07:02.450
and very protracted legal
case that some people fought

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:05.170
in order to prevent
being moved to the site.

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:06.740
They wanted to move up to a new site

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.770
on their ancestral land above the dam

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:12.390
all the way through to
the inundation phase

00:07:12.390 --> 00:07:14.170
from about 2015 onwards.

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:16.040
And now I'm looking at
the post inundation phase

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.410
where you've got these people

00:07:17.410 --> 00:07:18.940
including three very small villages

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.970
that are in fact now above
the dam as they were hoping

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.100
who are really struggling
to kind of create new

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:27.290
and livable futures for
themselves in this new space

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:30.690
where actually there was no real...

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.180
They weren't really meant
to exist in these spaces,

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.400
in these kinds of brand new
wooden villages that were built

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:39.080
above a major development project.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.480
They were never meant to be there.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:41.710
And so it's been really interesting

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:43.790
kind of writing about these experiences.

00:07:43.790 --> 00:07:45.620
And I'm now trying to
work on my second book

00:07:45.620 --> 00:07:48.170
which will be a sort of
more than human ethnography

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:52.090
of how these different effected villages

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.210
try to navigate their
relationships with each other,

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.510
but also with a developmental state

00:07:56.510 --> 00:07:59.690
with various indigenous
rights activists and lawyers

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.630
who got involved in the case

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:02.980
with different Christian churches

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:04.040
all had very different kinds

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:07.210
of spiritual and political
stakes in this situation.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.760
And also with place spirits
and with the changing landscape

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:11.040
and with the Christian god.

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:13.600
I mean, these were all
very sort of active players

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:14.590
during my field work.

00:08:14.590 --> 00:08:17.420
And I've found a kind of
more than human lands,

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:18.840
a very useful way of thinking

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:21.220
through these extremely sort of visceral,

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:23.910
very sort of material experiences.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:26.800
So that as opposed to a bit,
and also my current work

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.050
but what I'm actually doing right now

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:33.310
is a sort of extension of
this earlier interest, I guess

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.323
in more than human anthropology,

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:41.220
but also the ways in which
rural and indigenous communities

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.050
in Borneo engage with global movements

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:47.300
and kind of entire complexes.

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:50.770
So at the moment I've
got a couple of projects

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:54.583
going on the global nexus
of orangutan conservation.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:57.950
The first one is a fairly sort of

00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:01.480
classic anthropological
multi-sided exploration

00:09:01.480 --> 00:09:05.510
of how the different bits,
people, things, ideas

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.210
of this conservation nexus
has kind of move around

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.750
shape each other, shape
realities on the ground

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:12.120
or very often fail to there.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:15.460
There are many tensions and
conflicts within this network,

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:16.543
as you can imagine.

00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:20.280
And so I've got a team of
researchers working with me.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:21.910
I can talk about that a little bit.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:23.760
And what we're trying to do is basically

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:25.870
use in-depth anthropological research

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.320
at different sites of
orangutan conservation,

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.160
ranging from little villages,

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.887
caught up in wild orangutan
conservation schemes

00:09:31.887 --> 00:09:34.430
in Central Kalimantan in Borneo

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:36.100
to orangutan charities in the UK

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:38.520
that are raising funds for these schemes.

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:39.610
And we're trying to sort of build up

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:40.870
a more kind of holistic picture

00:09:40.870 --> 00:09:44.200
of how this one conservation
nexus is or isn't working

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.270
against the backdrop of the Anthropocene.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:48.520
And that's a whole other discussion

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:49.710
that we can have later on.

00:09:49.710 --> 00:09:50.870
So that's one project.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:55.870
The second project is
slightly more unusual

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:57.530
I think for an anthropologist,

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.160
it's a more sort of applied anthropology

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:01.890
conservation collaboration

00:10:01.890 --> 00:10:03.270
where I'm working with Eric Meijaard

00:10:03.270 --> 00:10:06.910
who's quite a senior orangutan
conservation scientist

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:10.810
and we're basically trying
to use ethnographic insights

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.950
and methods to devise better ways

00:10:12.950 --> 00:10:16.560
of mitigating orangutan
killing in rural Borneo.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.740
And more importantly for me to
try and help conservationist

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.540
to engage better with local communities

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:25.700
in more kind of
contextually specific ways.

00:10:25.700 --> 00:10:29.500
So those are kind of the three main bits

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:30.870
of my research trajectory.

00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:33.610
I mean, in terms of the core themes

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:35.187
I think maybe I've
already touched on them.

00:10:35.187 --> 00:10:37.371
There's a sort of enduring interest I have

00:10:37.371 --> 00:10:39.750
in more than human interactions.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:42.030
And by this, I include
everything from objects

00:10:42.030 --> 00:10:45.540
and plants and animals to
spirits that you can't see.

00:10:45.540 --> 00:10:47.760
And I think I'm also very interested

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.580
in trying to use a sort
of grounded perspective,

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.260
in-depth ethnographic research
that takes local concepts

00:10:54.260 --> 00:10:56.130
and experiences seriously

00:10:56.130 --> 00:10:59.950
as a way of cutting these big ideas

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.320
like the Anthropocene or like development

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.860
or Christianity down to size

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:07.040
and to try and understand
the kinds of local faces

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:08.870
that they acquire on the ground.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:10.477
So, yeah, that's a sort of quick...

00:11:10.477 --> 00:11:12.927
That's a really quick
overview of what I'm doing.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:17.300
- I was muted.

00:11:17.300 --> 00:11:19.640
So fascinating to hear you just walk us

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.760
through the broad trajectory
of all your research.

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:26.270
There's so many tangents
that are all entangled

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.233
around these core ideas
of the more than human.

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.460
I'd actually like to hear
you talk a little bit

00:11:32.460 --> 00:11:34.570
about what you see the contribution

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:38.520
of more than human anthropology doing,

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:42.400
because it's rising as a field these days

00:11:42.400 --> 00:11:44.520
but I also would like to ask you

00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:46.530
because in some of your writing,

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:50.830
you developed some critiques
of the ontological turn

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:54.250
and let's mindful of the audience

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:56.450
not everyone in the
audience will necessarily

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:58.780
be an anthropologist per se.

00:11:58.780 --> 00:12:02.270
And so if you could explain
the connection to your critique

00:12:02.270 --> 00:12:05.830
of the ontological turn, sort
of situate that a little bit,

00:12:05.830 --> 00:12:08.750
explain what you think they're doing

00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:11.910
and why you're a little bit hesitant

00:12:11.910 --> 00:12:13.110
about some of the aspects,

00:12:13.110 --> 00:12:17.180
but also inspired it seems to
me by them a little bit too.

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.150
And then also talk a little bit

00:12:19.150 --> 00:12:20.520
about what you think the importance

00:12:20.520 --> 00:12:24.083
of the more than human turn
in anthropology is too.

00:12:25.646 --> 00:12:27.530
It's a lot. So you can separate.

00:12:27.530 --> 00:12:29.970
You can talk about only one
of them if you want, but-

00:12:29.970 --> 00:12:31.780
- Well, I mean, I'll start
with the ontological turn

00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:33.310
but just kind of give me a push

00:12:33.310 --> 00:12:35.130
if I forget about the other bits.

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:35.963
- Sure.

00:12:35.963 --> 00:12:38.050
- Yeah. So, wow.

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:38.883
Where do I start?

00:12:38.883 --> 00:12:40.470
Okay, so the ontological turn

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:44.260
is essentially a sort of movement.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.580
Well let's call it an umbrella term

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:47.660
that draws together a bunch

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.470
of very different approaches that emerged

00:12:49.470 --> 00:12:51.360
in and beyond anthropology,

00:12:51.360 --> 00:12:53.500
across the social sciences and humanities

00:12:53.500 --> 00:12:55.780
around the late 2000s
or mid to late 2000s.

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:59.720
And that was most strongly articulated

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:03.920
by a group of scholars in about
the late 2000s, early 2010s

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.270
particularly a group of anthropologists

00:13:05.270 --> 00:13:09.270
from Cambridge at the
time who actually came up

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.370
with the phrase, the ontological turn.

00:13:11.370 --> 00:13:12.770
So what they did was they
kind of drew together

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:14.720
all of these different developments,

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:16.110
which were connected as they saw it

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:17.810
and gave it a name and said,

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:20.250
this is the future of anthropology.

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:24.740
And so essentially what
these anthropologists argued

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.460
at the time was that up to that point,

00:13:28.460 --> 00:13:32.680
anthropologists had failed
to really take seriously.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:34.653
We use this term or not take seriously,

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:39.730
the various beliefs, political concepts,

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:43.800
experiences of the people
that they worked with

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:48.800
because they were unable
to apprehend these beliefs

00:13:49.590 --> 00:13:51.940
and concepts and practices and so on

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:53.320
on ontological turns.

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:57.430
which is to say that if, for
example you saw a bunch of...

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:01.030
If somebody told you that
shape is my cape is my ancestor

00:14:01.030 --> 00:14:04.790
or is that power, that powder is powerful.

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:08.730
You as the kind of Cartesian
rational anthropologists

00:14:08.730 --> 00:14:11.120
would say, well that's just a kind of...

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:15.234
That's a cultural gloss
of an ontological reality.

00:14:15.234 --> 00:14:16.900
That's not really power.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:21.510
That's just powder that my
informants believes is power,

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.920
or that cape is not really an ancestor.

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:25.290
That's just the kind of...

00:14:25.290 --> 00:14:26.540
That's just a material object.

00:14:26.540 --> 00:14:29.690
It's just that they believe
that this is their ancestor.

00:14:29.690 --> 00:14:31.520
And so proponents of the ontological turn

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.121
basically argued that this
sort of smarter than that

00:14:34.121 --> 00:14:37.580
as one of them put a tendency was actually

00:14:38.617 --> 00:14:41.440
not a very ethical way
of doing anthropology

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:43.440
but also not a very
conceptually creative way

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:44.350
of doing anthropology

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:45.940
because you weren't
then always going to be

00:14:45.940 --> 00:14:50.000
sort of hamstrung by your own
kind of ontological schema

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:51.100
and your own assumptions,

00:14:51.100 --> 00:14:52.670
which meant that it would be impossible

00:14:52.670 --> 00:14:57.580
to fully engage with these
different people and concepts

00:14:57.580 --> 00:14:58.990
on their own terms.

00:14:58.990 --> 00:15:01.820
And so in place of what they saw is,

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:03.017
is very inadequate concept.

00:15:03.017 --> 00:15:04.120
And I should say that I don't think

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:05.500
they were entirely fair either

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:07.530
about sort of criticizing
previous generations

00:15:07.530 --> 00:15:09.690
for not taking people seriously,

00:15:09.690 --> 00:15:13.010
but in place of what they
saw as this kind of flawed

00:15:13.010 --> 00:15:15.620
approach to anthropology, they took

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:17.950
a sort of deliberately naive approach.

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:21.360
Which basically aimed to take
the interlocutors concepts,

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.640
words, beliefs at face
value on ontological turns.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.770
So if you say that cape is your ancestor

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.830
in your ethnography you
say you don't write.

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:33.750
They believe that that
cape is their ancestor.

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:35.800
You just say that cape is their ancestor

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:36.710
and you run with it.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.313
And then from that point
on, once you've kind of made

00:15:39.313 --> 00:15:42.460
that sort of ontological
leap and started to engage

00:15:42.460 --> 00:15:47.100
with these local other
radically different concepts

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:49.904
on their own terms, then
the whole sort of process

00:15:49.904 --> 00:15:53.410
of conceptual creativity
and theoretical creativity

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:54.243
could begin.

00:15:54.243 --> 00:15:56.270
And that was for
ontological turn proponents

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:58.560
that was the kind of most
exciting bit of anthropology.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:01.950
That point where you confronted
this radical alterity

00:16:01.950 --> 00:16:04.360
you had your own presumption shaken up,

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:07.830
and then you came to this new
and exciting understanding

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:10.220
of the world or this concept
or belief or whatever

00:16:10.220 --> 00:16:11.850
and then where she were off, right.

00:16:11.850 --> 00:16:13.470
And then you could sort of
talk to other anthropologists

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:14.430
about what you've found.

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:16.290
So that's essentially it.

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:21.290
So I think in many ways
the ontological turn

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:26.030
does provide a very useful
kind of methodological

00:16:26.030 --> 00:16:28.060
and analytical approach to anthropologists

00:16:28.060 --> 00:16:29.610
especially of Southeast Asia,

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:32.800
where as you know there's a
huge amount of heterogeneity

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:35.070
that there's so many different
sort of linguistic, cultural,

00:16:35.070 --> 00:16:37.000
religious complex has so
many different kinds of

00:16:37.000 --> 00:16:40.870
social formations, politics and so on,

00:16:40.870 --> 00:16:43.730
that are also co-existing
with very, very strong

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:46.240
and very influential homogenizing forces

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.660
like states, nationalist movements,

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:53.040
sort of Orthodox Islam,
Orthodox Christianity

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.830
all of which have a sort of tendency

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:56.640
to suppress that heterogeneity

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:59.560
and have a tendency to just kind of squash

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:01.050
that sort of difference in the interest

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:04.240
of creating a more kind of
purified or unified whole.

00:17:04.240 --> 00:17:06.490
And I think in that sense,

00:17:06.490 --> 00:17:10.870
maintaining that sort of
ontological turny impulse

00:17:10.870 --> 00:17:12.120
I guess, ontological turny.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:12.953
Yeah. Anyway.

00:17:12.953 --> 00:17:15.550
That impulse to kind of push back

00:17:15.550 --> 00:17:17.050
against homogenizing forces,

00:17:17.050 --> 00:17:18.580
to avoid explaining things away

00:17:18.580 --> 00:17:22.300
by recourse to your own
concepts and your own values

00:17:22.300 --> 00:17:25.150
can be extremely useful to
anthropologists of Southeast Asia

00:17:25.150 --> 00:17:26.580
as we grapple with all these kinds

00:17:26.580 --> 00:17:29.940
of different possibilities,
different life worlds,

00:17:29.940 --> 00:17:32.270
just kind of different
ontologies you might argue.

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:33.103
And actually here,

00:17:33.103 --> 00:17:35.640
I think we might maybe
learn a thing or two

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:37.820
from people like Marisol de la Cadena,

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:39.030
another sort of contributor

00:17:39.030 --> 00:17:42.140
to more ontologically
inflicted anthropology

00:17:42.140 --> 00:17:44.630
with her work on indigenous
cosmic politics in the Andes

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:47.570
where she does in fact write to adopter

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:51.240
to kind of engage with these
radically different concepts

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:52.073
on their own terms.

00:17:52.073 --> 00:17:53.210
So that's the kind of...

00:17:53.210 --> 00:17:54.929
That's a very quick summary
of the ontological turn

00:17:54.929 --> 00:17:58.010
and why it might be useful
to Southeast Asianist

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.260
but as Eric, you noted

00:18:00.260 --> 00:18:03.130
I do have some concerns
with the ontological turn.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:05.680
So I'm gonna try and
summarize them really quickly.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:11.750
I think my first concern
and is that even though,

00:18:12.300 --> 00:18:14.470
let's call them ontologists
just for short, right?

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:17.280
Even though ontologists
do say quite explicitly

00:18:17.280 --> 00:18:19.560
that they're not trying to

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:21.840
sort of reify otherness and difference,

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.220
then they're not going
out there looking for

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:26.890
sort of noble savages or
exotic eithers or whatever.

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:30.000
They're saying it's about
anything that's unfamiliar

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:31.220
or different that sort of challenges

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:33.550
your preconceived notions,
coulees or alterity.

00:18:33.550 --> 00:18:36.850
So even though they're
not explicitly saying that

00:18:36.850 --> 00:18:40.370
they're looking for a
radically cultural other,

00:18:40.370 --> 00:18:42.570
I think in practice,

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:45.100
what an ontological approach tends to do

00:18:45.100 --> 00:18:49.090
is replicate a dichotomy that has

00:18:49.090 --> 00:18:52.010
kind of haunted anthropology
since its inception

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:54.230
which is that dichotomy
between the sort of

00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:57.460
Western implicitly
Cartesian Euro-American self

00:18:57.460 --> 00:19:02.380
with all its baggage
and the cultural other.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:05.330
So when you start to read quite carefully

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:07.980
what ontologists are writing,

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:10.090
even though they never
explicitly talk about

00:19:10.090 --> 00:19:14.640
this being about otherness and
radically different people,

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:16.670
their work is implicitly addressed

00:19:16.670 --> 00:19:21.150
towards a we that is composed of people

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:22.010
who are seemed to have

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:23.550
a kind of Western Cartesian background.

00:19:23.550 --> 00:19:27.620
Anthropologists for whom that
kind of leap of understanding

00:19:28.930 --> 00:19:30.390
it's really, really difficult

00:19:30.390 --> 00:19:33.460
because they're facing such
radically other concepts

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:34.680
and those radically other concepts

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.940
always seem to be anti Cartesian,

00:19:36.940 --> 00:19:40.780
or just kind of these
conceptual foils to Western...

00:19:40.780 --> 00:19:42.210
To a very hackneyed understanding

00:19:42.210 --> 00:19:44.370
of what Western thought is.

00:19:44.370 --> 00:19:46.660
As you can imagine, I felt personally

00:19:46.660 --> 00:19:48.100
quite uncomfortable with this as well

00:19:48.100 --> 00:19:50.810
as a sort of Singaporean anthropologist

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:52.490
who didn't necessarily see myself

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:56.370
or kind of recognize
myself as part of that us,

00:19:56.370 --> 00:19:57.330
as opposed to that them.

00:19:57.330 --> 00:20:01.150
So when I was doing field work
in the village for example,

00:20:01.150 --> 00:20:03.270
when people talked about
spirits living in trees

00:20:03.270 --> 00:20:06.460
and little demons that
could suck your blood,

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:08.554
all these things I've
grown up with this stuff.

00:20:08.554 --> 00:20:12.350
It was not a huge conceptual
leap for me to understand

00:20:12.350 --> 00:20:14.130
what was going on and to
take this stuff seriously.

00:20:14.130 --> 00:20:15.660
I didn't need to explain these way.

00:20:15.660 --> 00:20:16.493
I mean, I felt...

00:20:16.493 --> 00:20:18.900
I got these shivers when people
talked about these things

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:20.530
because I recognize them.

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:21.760
So I think there is that problem

00:20:21.760 --> 00:20:24.840
that even though they don't
necessarily explicitly try to

00:20:24.840 --> 00:20:26.570
there is a tendency to kind of fall back

00:20:26.570 --> 00:20:30.170
on this rather problematic
dichotomy within anthropology.

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.030
And I guess sort of linked to that,

00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:35.060
is that I do have some
concerns with the position

00:20:35.060 --> 00:20:38.593
and the role of the anthropologist
in the ontological turn.

00:20:40.473 --> 00:20:42.870
And this was very much
the basis of my critique

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:46.470
in the Troubled landscapes
paper that you read Eric,

00:20:46.470 --> 00:20:49.340
where what you're essentially doing

00:20:49.340 --> 00:20:52.180
by kind of privileging that encounter

00:20:52.180 --> 00:20:54.020
between the anthropologist

00:20:54.020 --> 00:20:56.070
and that moment of radical alterity

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:59.020
that sort of unexplainable,
unfamiliar moment

00:20:59.020 --> 00:21:02.550
where your whole world
gets kind of blown apart.

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:03.750
What you're kind of doing,

00:21:03.750 --> 00:21:06.680
I think is inadvertently
centering the anthropologist.

00:21:06.680 --> 00:21:08.360
And you're basically
turning the anthropologist

00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:11.610
into this kind of rather
invulnerable, impermeable arbiter

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:16.980
who has the complete
capacity and thus authority

00:21:16.980 --> 00:21:19.160
to decide which concepts, which beliefs

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:22.110
should and shouldn't be
treated on ontological turns.

00:21:22.110 --> 00:21:24.580
Which of these you're gonna run with

00:21:24.580 --> 00:21:29.580
as the basis of your kind of
conceptual creative process.

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:31.520
And I guess my problem with this is that

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:34.120
it tends to sort of purify
the field work and counter.

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:37.920
It sort of, it strips
away all the confusion,

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:40.640
the mistranslation, the misunderstandings,

00:21:40.640 --> 00:21:44.000
the tensions that you inevitably
encounter in the field.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:46.590
There's so many times when
I've been told something

00:21:46.590 --> 00:21:47.423
by my friends,

00:21:47.423 --> 00:21:48.300
I've taken it really seriously.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:49.350
I've written it down.

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:50.440
And then the next day they're like,

00:21:50.440 --> 00:21:51.930
oh, by the way that was just a joke.

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:53.410
You didn't really believe that. Did you?

00:21:53.410 --> 00:21:54.940
That thing about spirits?

00:21:54.940 --> 00:21:58.570
And you get so much of this in the field

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:00.320
or you get people kind of
deliberately pulling you

00:22:00.320 --> 00:22:02.760
into their own political projects

00:22:02.760 --> 00:22:04.330
and kind of making up all sorts of things

00:22:04.330 --> 00:22:06.699
that will boost their
position, whatever, right?

00:22:06.699 --> 00:22:11.140
So there are lots of these
kinds of little messes

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:13.730
and complications of
co-presence as I call it

00:22:13.730 --> 00:22:16.270
that you will find within
the actual experience

00:22:16.270 --> 00:22:18.900
of field work that kind
of gets stripped away

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:21.470
when you start trying to approach things

00:22:21.470 --> 00:22:23.690
in this deliberately naive manner.

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:25.390
And so what I guess I was calling for

00:22:25.390 --> 00:22:27.960
in this Troubled landscapes paper

00:22:27.960 --> 00:22:32.830
was basically more sort
of more humble approach

00:22:32.830 --> 00:22:36.890
to anthropology that didn't
just kind of shine a spotlight

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:37.980
on the anthropologist

00:22:37.980 --> 00:22:40.670
and kind of privileged the
anthropologist as the locus

00:22:40.670 --> 00:22:43.330
of all that was exciting and anthropology.

00:22:43.330 --> 00:22:44.860
Once you've got these wonderful concepts

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:47.500
off you go, go talk to your
mates in the ivory tower

00:22:47.500 --> 00:22:49.320
and kind of make up new theories, great.

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:50.970
That was precisely what
I was trying to avoid.

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:51.803
And I was...

00:22:51.803 --> 00:22:52.636
What I was basically saying was no,

00:22:52.636 --> 00:22:54.610
we need to try and ground these encounters

00:22:54.610 --> 00:22:57.380
in the networks of
co-presence in the field,

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:00.187
which we can't afford to
erase from our ethnographies

00:23:00.187 --> 00:23:05.187
and of which we are inevitably
part as anthropologists.

00:23:05.390 --> 00:23:07.870
So that's kind of where my discomfort

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:10.170
with the ontological turn lies.

00:23:10.170 --> 00:23:11.003
So that was-

00:23:11.003 --> 00:23:11.950
- No, that's really...

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:13.020
This is fascinating.

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:14.710
The thing that I find so fascinating

00:23:14.710 --> 00:23:17.710
about your career and
what you've just described

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:20.320
is that your critique
of the ontological turn

00:23:21.457 --> 00:23:25.110
"Takes seriously" the impulse
of the ontological turn,

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:27.730
which they claim in some ways

00:23:27.730 --> 00:23:31.730
is to take seriously other perspectives,

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:33.610
but then you recognize in the process

00:23:33.610 --> 00:23:35.780
it's inadvertently privileged
the anthropologists.

00:23:35.780 --> 00:23:37.870
So it's a very interesting critique

00:23:37.870 --> 00:23:42.280
that is sensitive to the
ambitions of the turn

00:23:42.280 --> 00:23:45.270
but also recognizing its
internal contradictions

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:46.780
which is quite interesting.

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:50.290
I wonder if you could take
us into Borneo a little bit

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:54.050
and in that essay where
you're talking about this

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:57.340
and describe a term that you use in there.

00:23:57.340 --> 00:24:00.959
You say there's a risk
in the ontological turn

00:24:00.959 --> 00:24:05.959
to have a proclivity to gloss
over local heterogeneities,

00:24:06.130 --> 00:24:07.920
which I found really gripping

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:09.970
because it reminded me
of my own field work

00:24:09.970 --> 00:24:12.960
in Southeast Asia where
there's lots of heterogeneities

00:24:12.960 --> 00:24:15.595
and whenever you want to
claim that there's a kind of

00:24:15.595 --> 00:24:18.980
an ontology of something
it can be quite tricky.

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:20.890
But I wondered if you
could describe a couple

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:25.620
of the heterogeneities that
maybe like let off a light bulb

00:24:25.620 --> 00:24:27.140
for you or something like that.

00:24:27.140 --> 00:24:27.973
And then after that

00:24:27.973 --> 00:24:30.270
we'll turn to talk about
your orangutan work.

00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:32.070
And then after that, we'll open it up

00:24:32.070 --> 00:24:34.700
to questions from the audience.

00:24:34.700 --> 00:24:35.533
- Okay, great.

00:24:35.533 --> 00:24:36.710
Thank you.

00:24:36.710 --> 00:24:37.543
Yeah, absolutely.

00:24:37.543 --> 00:24:40.580
So this kind of goes back to
my earlier point, I guess,

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:45.450
about how ontologists kind of hold,

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:49.300
I guess they privilege
that moment of encounter

00:24:49.300 --> 00:24:51.630
between the anthropologist
and the radical other,

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:52.850
the alterity.

00:24:52.850 --> 00:24:54.780
And that's kind of like the moment,

00:24:54.780 --> 00:24:57.000
when everything gets really exciting.

00:24:57.000 --> 00:24:58.270
So you kind of...

00:24:58.270 --> 00:25:02.220
You would often hear
ontologists saying in person,

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:03.600
I'm not really that interested

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:06.278
in the kind of boring everyday stuff.

00:25:06.278 --> 00:25:07.118
I just...

00:25:07.118 --> 00:25:09.390
I'm just really interested
in that unusual moment

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:11.780
because that for me is
the most important thing.

00:25:11.780 --> 00:25:13.400
And I guess one of...

00:25:14.383 --> 00:25:15.216
No shoots.

00:25:15.216 --> 00:25:19.180
So one of the consequences
of keeping that encounter,

00:25:19.180 --> 00:25:21.770
that interface between
anthropologists and other

00:25:21.770 --> 00:25:23.670
at the forefront of the ontological turn

00:25:23.670 --> 00:25:25.860
is that that whole other bit gets squashed

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:27.080
in the same way that the whole sort

00:25:27.080 --> 00:25:28.680
of anthropologist bit
gets squashed, right?

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:31.820
So there's another sort of
edited volume I recently did

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:33.730
called Who are we? Which is...

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:35.570
Which was very much
about trying to question

00:25:35.570 --> 00:25:40.450
this rather on unthinking,
unproblematic invocation

00:25:40.450 --> 00:25:44.510
of the sort of anthropological
we, as the imagined audience

00:25:44.510 --> 00:25:46.810
who exactly are this we
that we're talking about?

00:25:46.810 --> 00:25:48.460
Anyway, that's anthropologist.

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:51.610
So we've got the sort of
local heterogeneity here.

00:25:51.610 --> 00:25:53.660
Yeah, so there were many,
many moments in the field

00:25:53.660 --> 00:25:57.470
where I sort of encountered
these sorts of divisions

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:00.578
and this was precisely what
made me quite suspicious

00:26:00.578 --> 00:26:02.380
of any sort of program that tried

00:26:02.380 --> 00:26:04.850
to make vast sweeping
statements about the Bidayuh

00:26:04.850 --> 00:26:06.490
or about indigenous people as a whole,

00:26:06.490 --> 00:26:07.690
because this was...

00:26:07.690 --> 00:26:12.690
It was I feel like a bit
of a kind of misery guts,

00:26:12.730 --> 00:26:15.480
but, you know, it's very, very difficult

00:26:15.480 --> 00:26:17.760
to kind of run with those projects

00:26:17.760 --> 00:26:20.440
when you're very aware of
the ethnographic complexity

00:26:20.440 --> 00:26:22.680
and the messiness of what's
going on in the ground.

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:24.440
So one example, which I mentioned

00:26:24.440 --> 00:26:29.150
was different Christian
views of the old people,

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:31.990
the old animus practitioners
and the old spirits

00:26:31.990 --> 00:26:33.800
that were still around,
which I talked about

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:35.810
in this other paper, Eric,

00:26:35.810 --> 00:26:38.650
which you read, To know or not to know?

00:26:38.650 --> 00:26:42.040
And this was a really
interesting sort of tension

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:43.320
that I encountered in the field.

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:45.270
Which was, what on earth do
you do with these old guys

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.970
who are still insisting on
practicing the old rituals?

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:51.110
How do you deal with the
kinds of spirit realities

00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:52.560
that these rituals bring to the village?

00:26:52.560 --> 00:26:55.820
Because the thing that
everybody understood

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:58.140
was that even though most people

00:26:58.140 --> 00:26:59.440
had converted to Christianity,

00:26:59.440 --> 00:27:01.750
the old spirits were still there.

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:02.670
You know they...

00:27:02.670 --> 00:27:05.350
As long as people continued
to believing in them,

00:27:05.350 --> 00:27:06.670
practicing the old rituals,

00:27:06.670 --> 00:27:08.850
doing things that would engage with them,

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.620
the old spirits would stay around.

00:27:10.620 --> 00:27:12.740
They were less powerful
and less influential now

00:27:12.740 --> 00:27:14.720
because everyone was,
most people were Christian

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:16.200
as they said, but they were there.

00:27:16.200 --> 00:27:17.600
And that was a problem

00:27:17.600 --> 00:27:20.340
because a lot of Christians
didn't necessarily

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:24.170
want to get embroiled in
relations with these old spirits.

00:27:24.170 --> 00:27:27.090
And so one of the
interesting things I found

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.090
was that Christians
views of the old spirits

00:27:30.090 --> 00:27:33.040
and the old rituals varied
according to their denomination

00:27:33.040 --> 00:27:35.992
which I wasn't expecting because
coming in from the outside

00:27:35.992 --> 00:27:38.930
I had a very kind of dim
view of missionaries.

00:27:38.930 --> 00:27:41.993
I just thought, yeah,
you know, nasty bunch,

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:44.670
foisting a foreign
religion on these people.

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:45.670
I mean, it turns out Christianity

00:27:45.670 --> 00:27:48.570
was really, really important
to their lives and that's fine.

00:27:48.570 --> 00:27:52.170
And what I found was that the
Anglicans and the Catholics

00:27:52.170 --> 00:27:53.560
whose missionaries had been there

00:27:53.560 --> 00:27:55.820
since the late 19th century

00:27:56.790 --> 00:27:59.470
but had never really converted
people very successfully

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:03.840
until about the 1960s and
'70s for various reasons

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:07.510
had a very sort of accommodating
view of the old rituals.

00:28:07.510 --> 00:28:10.290
As far as they were concerned,

00:28:10.290 --> 00:28:13.010
the old rituals worship the same god.

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.620
So they used the same term, the Bau

00:28:14.620 --> 00:28:17.400
which was the Bau was the
animus word for the Supreme god.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:20.280
And the Bau has become the sort of

00:28:20.280 --> 00:28:22.633
Catholic and Anglican
word for god the Father.

00:28:23.976 --> 00:28:26.700
The practitioners weren't
doing anything bad.

00:28:26.700 --> 00:28:27.900
I mean, if you were doing anything bad,

00:28:27.900 --> 00:28:29.280
you'd go into a lot of trouble

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:30.980
'cause people were very
aware of kind of black magic,

00:28:30.980 --> 00:28:32.400
but as far as they were concerned,

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:35.260
most of the old rituals were
designed to bring good things

00:28:35.260 --> 00:28:37.500
to the village and bless
the village's inhabitants.

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:39.300
So no big deal, right?

00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:41.510
You just sort of carry on helping them.

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:45.350
And they also saw it as an
important kind of familial

00:28:45.350 --> 00:28:50.350
or neighborly obligation to
help these older villages.

00:28:50.350 --> 00:28:51.940
So it was very much a way of kind of

00:28:51.940 --> 00:28:54.360
cementing their existing social ties

00:28:54.360 --> 00:28:56.420
with people who as far as they
were concerned needed help.

00:28:56.420 --> 00:28:59.990
So anytime you went to one of
these rituals in the village

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:02.710
you would find your 10
animus practitioners

00:29:02.710 --> 00:29:05.560
doing their thing, doing the
main chance and the offerings

00:29:05.560 --> 00:29:06.810
and dancers and everything,

00:29:06.810 --> 00:29:08.640
but all around them would be Christians.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:09.690
And the Christians would be there

00:29:09.690 --> 00:29:12.260
building the altars for
them, going up to the jungle

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:15.050
to collect the bamboo that
they needed for these things,

00:29:15.050 --> 00:29:17.776
cooking the offerings,
killing the sacrificial pigs,

00:29:17.776 --> 00:29:19.900
playing the gongs, everything.

00:29:19.900 --> 00:29:21.500
And they saw this as a
really important part

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:23.620
of their kind of duty.

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:26.740
The kind of the people
who didn't agree with this

00:29:26.740 --> 00:29:31.040
were members of the evangelical church.

00:29:31.040 --> 00:29:32.880
So this is the Sidang Injil Borneo.

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:34.130
It's a sort of homegrown

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:35.930
non-denominational evangelical church

00:29:35.930 --> 00:29:37.530
which something you've heard of.

00:29:38.870 --> 00:29:41.550
These guys arrived in
the village in the 1990s

00:29:41.550 --> 00:29:43.420
and the first converts were converts

00:29:43.420 --> 00:29:44.920
from Anglicanism and Catholicism.

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:46.840
So there was already
a bit of tension there

00:29:46.840 --> 00:29:49.490
in that the church was
seen as kind of poaching

00:29:49.490 --> 00:29:51.570
some of the existing Christians
and making them convert

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:54.023
to a different sort of Christianity.

00:29:55.140 --> 00:29:55.973
They had a very...

00:29:55.973 --> 00:29:57.740
They had a much more
kind of puritanical view

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:59.920
of what was and wasn't acceptable.

00:29:59.920 --> 00:30:04.420
No drinking, no smoking,
no gambling which was big.

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:06.530
It was a big sort of
lubricant of sociality

00:30:06.530 --> 00:30:09.267
in the village with all the
Catholics and Anglicans did.

00:30:09.267 --> 00:30:11.710
And they were really terrified.

00:30:11.710 --> 00:30:13.180
I mean, I think they were
actually quite scared

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:14.930
of the old rituals in the
sense that they saw them

00:30:14.930 --> 00:30:17.170
as the work of Satan that
would bring evil spirits

00:30:17.170 --> 00:30:18.380
into the village.

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:20.920
And so whenever these rituals took place,

00:30:20.920 --> 00:30:21.780
there was always trouble

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:25.210
because any time the old practitioners

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:28.290
came into contact with
the SIB, the evangelicals

00:30:29.250 --> 00:30:31.660
they get a really sort
of aggressive reception

00:30:31.660 --> 00:30:33.127
and many evangelicals would often try

00:30:33.127 --> 00:30:34.544
and sort of convert them

00:30:34.544 --> 00:30:36.570
and tell them that they
were going to hell.

00:30:36.570 --> 00:30:38.620
And their souls would not get

00:30:38.620 --> 00:30:41.350
to heaven and it was
all gonna be horrible.

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:43.850
And so that was one
really interesting kind of

00:30:43.850 --> 00:30:46.260
local heterogeneity that I
hadn't really expected it

00:30:46.260 --> 00:30:50.180
to come across when I was
actually doing field work.

00:30:50.180 --> 00:30:52.930
But which became quite a defining feature

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:55.890
of my understanding of Christianity.

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:57.970
And of course the same thing applies

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:00.040
to what people thought of the old spirits.

00:31:00.040 --> 00:31:03.140
Nobody could decide what
would actually happen

00:31:03.140 --> 00:31:05.590
to the old spirits and how
exactly to engage with them.

00:31:05.590 --> 00:31:09.090
So there was some people who...

00:31:09.090 --> 00:31:10.930
Especially the older
ones, if they'd grown up

00:31:10.930 --> 00:31:14.030
knowing how to deal with the old spirits

00:31:14.030 --> 00:31:17.170
if the encountered them in
the forest or in the village,

00:31:17.170 --> 00:31:18.680
they would just do the old things

00:31:18.680 --> 00:31:21.120
and then say a Christian prayer
at the end just to be safe.

00:31:21.120 --> 00:31:23.810
There were younger adults
who would do a kind of

00:31:23.810 --> 00:31:26.700
half-hearted response as they should

00:31:26.700 --> 00:31:28.580
and then say a prayer just to be safe.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:31.170
And then there were younger
adults who just refuse to know

00:31:31.170 --> 00:31:32.290
in many cases.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:35.520
They actually actively
cultivated a kind of agnosis

00:31:35.520 --> 00:31:40.250
or kind of not knowing precisely
because they were really...

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:43.670
They really didn't want to
sort of end up acquiring

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:45.240
the sort of knowledge
that would be necessary

00:31:45.240 --> 00:31:47.450
for them to engage in these
relations with the old spirits.

00:31:47.450 --> 00:31:50.750
Because the principle was once you knew

00:31:50.750 --> 00:31:52.700
what to do if a spirit
tried to talk to you

00:31:52.700 --> 00:31:54.840
or send you a message and
you didn't actually do it

00:31:54.840 --> 00:31:57.000
you were gonna be in really deep trouble.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:00.390
And so a lot of the
younger people were like,

00:32:00.390 --> 00:32:02.290
well, actually, you know what?

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:03.790
I don't wanna know about this.

00:32:05.150 --> 00:32:06.400
Which in turn meant they
never wanted to talk to me

00:32:06.400 --> 00:32:07.233
about it.

00:32:07.233 --> 00:32:09.180
So I'd be there with my notebook.

00:32:09.180 --> 00:32:11.040
Oh my god, you wouldn't
believe what I've just heard

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:12.750
about this and that the old people

00:32:12.750 --> 00:32:13.680
didn't tell me that much either

00:32:13.680 --> 00:32:15.168
'cause they didn't wanna
get me into trouble, right.

00:32:15.168 --> 00:32:17.900
They were like, ah, you're
already useless, right?

00:32:17.900 --> 00:32:19.010
You can't weave baskets.

00:32:19.010 --> 00:32:20.780
You can't plant rice very well.

00:32:20.780 --> 00:32:22.270
I'm not gonna tell you how
to deal with these spirits.

00:32:22.270 --> 00:32:23.103
No, no, no no.

00:32:23.103 --> 00:32:25.470
I'm just gonna tell you
really sort of generic stuff.

00:32:25.470 --> 00:32:26.680
So I write down very generic stuff.

00:32:26.680 --> 00:32:28.280
So they were kind of protecting me,

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.310
but the moment I go and talk
to some of the younger people

00:32:30.310 --> 00:32:33.630
they're just be like, I don't
wanna know, not interested.

00:32:33.630 --> 00:32:35.980
So there was a whole kind of spectrum

00:32:35.980 --> 00:32:37.500
of different ways of relating

00:32:37.500 --> 00:32:40.060
or in this case actually not
relating to the old spirits.

00:32:40.060 --> 00:32:42.060
That again, it's really interesting stuff

00:32:42.060 --> 00:32:45.120
but if you take a sort of
ontological turn approach to it,

00:32:45.120 --> 00:32:46.440
you're not gonna capture any of this stuff

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:47.273
because you're gonna be running

00:32:47.273 --> 00:32:49.530
with the most exciting points

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:51.500
where it's about the spirit concept.

00:32:51.500 --> 00:32:53.710
And then you kind of run
off with this concept

00:32:53.710 --> 00:32:56.252
but you're not gonna
capture that heterogeneity.

00:32:56.252 --> 00:32:57.890
- That's really fascinating.

00:32:57.890 --> 00:33:00.333
In some ways you've foreclosed
what you're able to look into

00:33:00.333 --> 00:33:02.453
because you're looking for alterity.

00:33:03.340 --> 00:33:06.060
Whereas the most important
story might be right there

00:33:06.060 --> 00:33:07.160
in front of your nose.

00:33:08.340 --> 00:33:10.090
We're running close to the time

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:12.370
I wanted to a lot to our conversation

00:33:12.370 --> 00:33:14.890
but I wanna make sure
that you say a few words

00:33:14.890 --> 00:33:18.750
about your new research
on orangutan conservation.

00:33:18.750 --> 00:33:21.040
So if you could speak for
a few minutes about that

00:33:21.040 --> 00:33:23.180
just to share that with our audience,

00:33:23.180 --> 00:33:25.698
and then after that, I'll turn in moderate

00:33:25.698 --> 00:33:28.750
open question and answer
with the rest of the people

00:33:28.750 --> 00:33:30.110
who've come today.

00:33:30.110 --> 00:33:31.200
- Yeah. Okay.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:34.440
So I think I very briefly touched on

00:33:34.440 --> 00:33:37.623
what my two projects in
orangutan conservation are doing.

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:40.630
Maybe what I could do
is share a little bit

00:33:40.630 --> 00:33:42.710
about what we're currently finding.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:46.070
So what we're basically in
the middle of these projects,

00:33:46.070 --> 00:33:49.630
most of us have kind of
finished most of our field work.

00:33:49.630 --> 00:33:51.260
There was a bit of
disruption due to COVID.

00:33:51.260 --> 00:33:52.190
So we're not quite there yet

00:33:52.190 --> 00:33:55.133
but we've got some really
interesting initial insights.

00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:59.920
So as I said, I've got one, two, three,

00:33:59.920 --> 00:34:01.530
three colleagues we're currently looking

00:34:01.530 --> 00:34:03.520
into human orangutan relations,

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:05.050
or actually the lag they're off

00:34:05.050 --> 00:34:07.030
on the ground in rural Borneo.

00:34:07.030 --> 00:34:08.300
And one of the really interesting things

00:34:08.300 --> 00:34:10.880
we found is this very marked contrast

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:13.841
between the sort of hyper
visibility of orangutans

00:34:13.841 --> 00:34:15.000
in the West

00:34:15.000 --> 00:34:16.320
as these very charismatic,

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:19.170
very famous icons of extinction, right?

00:34:19.170 --> 00:34:22.403
You think extinction,
rainforests, orangutans again.

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:26.690
And then on the other side,

00:34:26.690 --> 00:34:28.760
and in places like Borneo and Sumatra,

00:34:28.760 --> 00:34:31.690
you've got the relative
invisibility and insignificance.

00:34:31.690 --> 00:34:34.730
And what we tend to find
is that for the most part,

00:34:34.730 --> 00:34:37.650
rural villages in Borneo especially,

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:39.260
we haven't done that
much work in Sumatra yet,

00:34:39.260 --> 00:34:43.090
but we're getting similar input
from people we know there.

00:34:43.090 --> 00:34:45.140
For the most part, villages
are just not interested

00:34:45.140 --> 00:34:46.535
in orangutans.

00:34:46.535 --> 00:34:48.948
They're kind of the things
that you might encounter

00:34:48.948 --> 00:34:51.060
in the forest occasionally,

00:34:51.060 --> 00:34:53.330
but they're not particularly special.

00:34:53.330 --> 00:34:55.160
They're not particularly
creatures that you wanna form

00:34:55.160 --> 00:34:56.730
any relations with.

00:34:56.730 --> 00:34:58.450
Every now and then they
turn into these pests

00:34:58.450 --> 00:35:00.120
because when it's durian season

00:35:00.120 --> 00:35:03.390
and the trees are all fruiting,
out come the orangutans.

00:35:03.390 --> 00:35:05.230
And so my PhD student, Paul actually

00:35:05.230 --> 00:35:07.680
had some really interesting kind of notes

00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:08.940
from his conversations with farmers.

00:35:08.940 --> 00:35:11.690
Apparently orangutans are
seen as particularly naughty

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:14.810
because when they raid durian trees,

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:16.040
they don't just eat the durians,

00:35:16.040 --> 00:35:17.790
which is completely understandable,

00:35:17.790 --> 00:35:19.510
villagers are eating durians too.

00:35:19.510 --> 00:35:21.700
They throw the unripe
durians onto the ground

00:35:21.700 --> 00:35:23.570
which is really antisocial.

00:35:23.570 --> 00:35:24.980
So, you know, they...

00:35:24.980 --> 00:35:26.440
So by and large peoples...

00:35:26.440 --> 00:35:28.740
They're not companion
species, let's put it this way

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:30.450
in any sense of the term.

00:35:30.450 --> 00:35:33.450
For the most part there was
just no interest in orangutans.

00:35:33.450 --> 00:35:36.730
They're occasional pests. You
don't like having them around.

00:35:36.730 --> 00:35:39.190
What is though is that, the main ways

00:35:39.190 --> 00:35:43.340
in which orangutans become
visible and significant to people

00:35:43.340 --> 00:35:46.070
in these places is through conservation.

00:35:46.070 --> 00:35:49.190
So in many ways, what we're
finding is that field work

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:51.190
actually involves very little sort of

00:35:51.190 --> 00:35:54.600
physical encounters with orangutans

00:35:54.600 --> 00:35:56.000
and very little kind of evidence

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:58.130
of human orangutan relations necessarily

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:01.350
apart from pet-keeping, which
I'll talk about in a second.

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:03.280
And actually much more...

00:36:03.280 --> 00:36:05.710
It's a much more to do
with villages relations

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:08.160
with conservationists
who are in many ways,

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:11.800
making the orangutans much
more kind of significant

00:36:11.800 --> 00:36:14.730
and in a way, much more dangerous

00:36:14.730 --> 00:36:15.900
than they used to be in the past,

00:36:15.900 --> 00:36:18.380
because orangutans are
protected and people are aware

00:36:18.380 --> 00:36:19.770
that they're protected
and that you could get

00:36:19.770 --> 00:36:24.520
into a lot of trouble for harming
or capturing an orangutan,

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:26.230
which for the most part,
you've just never seen

00:36:26.230 --> 00:36:29.800
as particularly important or
special or worth protecting.

00:36:29.800 --> 00:36:32.160
And so what we're kind
of seeing emerge here

00:36:32.160 --> 00:36:36.130
is quite a significant amount of tension

00:36:36.130 --> 00:36:38.980
between people in Borneo especially

00:36:39.930 --> 00:36:43.410
and conservationists
who are very often seen

00:36:43.410 --> 00:36:46.580
as caring more about animals than people.

00:36:46.580 --> 00:36:48.670
So you kind of hear things like,

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:50.500
well we are the real orangutan.

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:53.510
Yeah. The real people of the forest.

00:36:53.510 --> 00:36:54.750
But why are they...

00:36:54.750 --> 00:36:57.570
Why are these foreigners
throwing all this money

00:36:57.570 --> 00:37:00.290
and care and everything

00:37:00.290 --> 00:37:02.780
at saving these kind of this one ape?

00:37:02.780 --> 00:37:04.510
Why just this one ape?

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:06.890
Why are you building these
huge rehabilitation centers

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:10.100
with state-of-the-art
x-rays, medical supplies,

00:37:10.100 --> 00:37:13.020
nappies, milk powder when we
don't even have this stuff?

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:16.580
So it's a really kind of
tense situation on the ground.

00:37:16.580 --> 00:37:17.810
There's a whole kind of spectrum

00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:19.930
of different conservationist as well.

00:37:19.930 --> 00:37:23.063
So that kind of adds to
the complexity of things.

00:37:24.280 --> 00:37:26.010
But I guess that that sort of tension

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:29.280
has really been the kind of
main feature of our field work

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:30.550
in these areas.

00:37:30.550 --> 00:37:32.340
I just wanna touch very
briefly on this issue

00:37:32.340 --> 00:37:33.320
of pet-keeping as well,

00:37:33.320 --> 00:37:35.663
which is in that article that I sent you.

00:37:37.410 --> 00:37:38.780
There is kind of one exception

00:37:38.780 --> 00:37:41.118
to this lack of interest in orangutans,

00:37:41.118 --> 00:37:43.480
or kind of antagonism towards orangutans.

00:37:43.480 --> 00:37:46.970
And that's pet-keeping,
that's when rural villages

00:37:46.970 --> 00:37:50.450
very often the mother orangutan is killed

00:37:50.450 --> 00:37:54.660
while trying to get fruit or
whatever or in somebody's farm.

00:37:54.660 --> 00:37:55.980
They take the baby as a pet.

00:37:55.980 --> 00:37:57.420
People are aware that you can sell the pet

00:37:57.420 --> 00:37:58.253
for quite a lot of money

00:37:58.253 --> 00:38:00.400
but some villages just
choose to keep pets.

00:38:02.180 --> 00:38:03.013
Yes, pets.

00:38:04.120 --> 00:38:05.730
And it's not very common
and it's quite dispersed,

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:06.680
but it does happen.

00:38:07.580 --> 00:38:09.950
And in these cases, what we tend to find

00:38:09.950 --> 00:38:11.700
is that these villages see themselves

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:15.420
as kind of carers of orangutans.

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:18.190
So they see themselves as
having save these poor babies

00:38:18.190 --> 00:38:19.023
who are completely helpless

00:38:19.023 --> 00:38:21.360
from a very cruel jungle
where they might get eaten

00:38:21.360 --> 00:38:24.490
or killed or died of starvation,
which is probably true.

00:38:24.490 --> 00:38:26.090
You know, they see themselves
as kind of investing

00:38:26.090 --> 00:38:28.630
into these creatures,
nurturing them, feeding them,

00:38:28.630 --> 00:38:31.310
paying money for them, buying
them nappies, whatever.

00:38:31.310 --> 00:38:32.150
And so they're not...

00:38:32.150 --> 00:38:34.220
As far as they're concerned,
they're not harming them.

00:38:34.220 --> 00:38:38.720
But then conservationists
become aware of this fact,

00:38:38.720 --> 00:38:41.030
this often results in quite a kind of

00:38:41.030 --> 00:38:43.650
dramatic orangutan confiscation operation

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:45.700
where these conservationists will turn up

00:38:47.160 --> 00:38:50.050
with members of the local
wildlife and forest authorities.

00:38:50.050 --> 00:38:51.416
'Cause there's always gonna
be a member of the government

00:38:51.416 --> 00:38:52.249
with them.

00:38:52.249 --> 00:38:54.540
They turn up, they
confiscate this orangutan.

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:55.373
They never...

00:38:55.373 --> 00:38:56.460
They don't offer compensation

00:38:56.460 --> 00:38:59.350
because as far as they're
concerned it's illegal.

00:38:59.350 --> 00:39:01.540
And then they take into
these rehabilitation centers

00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:03.070
where they get cared for,

00:39:03.070 --> 00:39:06.170
they get fed, they get
cuddled, they get nurtured.

00:39:06.170 --> 00:39:08.470
As far as villages are
concerned in much the same way

00:39:08.470 --> 00:39:10.750
as they were doing it in the village.

00:39:10.750 --> 00:39:11.667
And so this is,

00:39:11.667 --> 00:39:16.590
one of those really interesting
kind of ethical dilemmas

00:39:16.590 --> 00:39:17.423
I think,

00:39:17.423 --> 00:39:21.270
that's been haunting
me during my research.

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:23.240
And so just very briefly,

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:25.921
I think the sort of three
main ethical concerns

00:39:25.921 --> 00:39:29.740
that kind of smash up
against each other here.

00:39:29.740 --> 00:39:34.060
One of them is the sort of
cultural relativist impulse

00:39:34.060 --> 00:39:37.280
that many anthropologists
animated by, right?

00:39:37.280 --> 00:39:39.690
I mean, my first reaction was like,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:41.780
well, why shouldn't these
people be allowed to have

00:39:41.780 --> 00:39:43.680
whatever relations they
want with orangutans?

00:39:43.680 --> 00:39:44.513
You know, why not?

00:39:44.513 --> 00:39:47.010
You know, this is
colonialism, this is bad.

00:39:47.010 --> 00:39:49.378
And then you call it the whole sort of,

00:39:49.378 --> 00:39:51.660
well which comes into this
sort of more than human turn

00:39:51.660 --> 00:39:53.110
which I realize I didn't
really talk about,

00:39:53.110 --> 00:39:55.010
but this is part of this
sort of multi-species turn

00:39:55.010 --> 00:39:55.843
in anthropology

00:39:55.843 --> 00:39:57.810
where you start to think
about animals as agents

00:39:57.810 --> 00:40:00.900
and animals as kind of
beings with affective ties

00:40:00.900 --> 00:40:03.390
to humans and you think,
well, okay, what if that,

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:05.030
you watched some of these pet videos,

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:06.130
pet confiscation videos,

00:40:06.130 --> 00:40:07.190
and the baby orangutan

00:40:07.190 --> 00:40:08.720
obviously doesn't want
to leave it's its owner

00:40:08.720 --> 00:40:11.070
and you think, oh, no, what about

00:40:11.070 --> 00:40:13.790
the feelings of this poor orangutan?

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:16.970
Why should it be put through
this traumatic separation?

00:40:16.970 --> 00:40:19.260
And then the third sort of problem is that

00:40:20.630 --> 00:40:22.290
the main problem is that in reality

00:40:22.290 --> 00:40:24.840
this sort of pet-keeping
can be extremely damaging.

00:40:25.830 --> 00:40:28.280
Villagers don't tend to know
how to take care of orangutans.

00:40:28.280 --> 00:40:32.210
They feed them rice,
condensed snow, chocolates.

00:40:32.210 --> 00:40:34.390
Very often, they end up
getting malnourished or ill.

00:40:34.390 --> 00:40:36.040
They become too big to control.

00:40:36.040 --> 00:40:40.490
They get chained up, chuffed
into really crappy boxes.

00:40:40.490 --> 00:40:41.790
And actually, in that case

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:43.180
I think the conservation is all right.

00:40:43.180 --> 00:40:45.030
They're actually saving the orangutan

00:40:45.030 --> 00:40:46.830
from what could be a
really, really nasty fate

00:40:46.830 --> 00:40:48.160
much later on.

00:40:48.160 --> 00:40:50.310
And there's no resolution
to these impulses,

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:52.620
but that's kind of one example of

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:55.210
the kinds of ethical
dilemmas that our research

00:40:55.210 --> 00:40:56.610
is also trying to foreground.

00:40:56.610 --> 00:40:59.260
Anyway, I've spoken a
lot, so I'm gonna stop.

00:40:59.260 --> 00:41:00.140
- Well, thank you.

00:41:00.140 --> 00:41:00.973
You've spoken a lot

00:41:00.973 --> 00:41:03.460
but you've given us a lot to think about,

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:05.960
and I'm sure that our audience
has a lot of questions

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:06.793
for you.

00:41:06.793 --> 00:41:08.350
So what we'll do here is I'll be...

00:41:08.350 --> 00:41:12.240
I'll moderate the questions
using the Zoom interface.

00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:15.200
So those of you who have any questions

00:41:15.200 --> 00:41:18.120
if you can use the Hand Raise function

00:41:18.120 --> 00:41:19.020
that will help me out.

00:41:19.020 --> 00:41:21.500
But if you can't use
the Hand Raise function

00:41:21.500 --> 00:41:25.020
turn on your camera and
wave your hand wildly

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:27.350
And I'll try my best to notice you.

00:41:27.350 --> 00:41:30.580
I see that Michael Dove has his hand up

00:41:30.580 --> 00:41:32.010
using the Hand Raise function.

00:41:32.010 --> 00:41:33.260
So we'll start with Michael.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:34.160
Go ahead, Michael.

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:38.610
- Thanks, Eric.

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:43.610
Fascinating chat, which
I've immensely enjoyed.

00:41:43.810 --> 00:41:46.540
I wonder if we could go
back right to the beginning

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:51.287
when you talked about
your work with the Bidayuh

00:41:52.310 --> 00:41:53.923
in transition.

00:41:55.150 --> 00:42:00.150
So I'm wondering were the
Bidayuh ever not in transition?

00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:06.500
But Borneo has a very rich history

00:42:06.500 --> 00:42:11.500
and it almost to talk about
transition and pre-transition

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:16.250
almost sounds like we're
talking about a period

00:42:16.250 --> 00:42:17.683
of no history.

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:23.333
- Yeah. Thank you.

00:42:23.333 --> 00:42:26.630
I mean, I should clarify
that I'm talking about

00:42:26.630 --> 00:42:28.710
one of many, many transitional periods

00:42:28.710 --> 00:42:30.950
that Bidayuhs have gone through.

00:42:30.950 --> 00:42:35.230
So people talked about it as
a significant period of change

00:42:35.230 --> 00:42:38.070
and that was kind of
the sort of local lingo

00:42:38.070 --> 00:42:40.440
that I was picking up on
this idea of borrowing

00:42:40.440 --> 00:42:44.330
which is really quite fundamental
sort of transformation

00:42:44.330 --> 00:42:45.220
of their society.

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:47.413
And it was quite interesting that,

00:42:48.890 --> 00:42:51.210
I mean absolutely from a kind
of analytical point of view,

00:42:51.210 --> 00:42:52.080
I'm completely with you.

00:42:52.080 --> 00:42:53.380
They've always been in transition

00:42:53.380 --> 00:42:57.730
and they've always engaged
with wider forces in the world

00:42:57.730 --> 00:43:02.090
that they engaged with
what a trade networks

00:43:02.090 --> 00:43:03.300
for centuries before that.

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:05.200
They were very much some
of the earliest supporters

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.650
of the Brook State, which was interesting.

00:43:06.650 --> 00:43:08.860
They've been some really
interesting sort of analysis

00:43:08.860 --> 00:43:11.400
of the relationships that they formed

00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:12.730
with the government

00:43:12.730 --> 00:43:14.330
and they took that the
British colonial government.

00:43:14.330 --> 00:43:16.660
So they've been going through
various stages of transition,

00:43:16.660 --> 00:43:17.493
I guess.

00:43:17.493 --> 00:43:19.460
I mean, maybe transition
isn't even the best word

00:43:19.460 --> 00:43:22.540
because this is very
much part of a continuum

00:43:22.540 --> 00:43:26.110
of engaging with various external,

00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:30.390
foreign, regional,
local forces and agents.

00:43:30.390 --> 00:43:34.070
The reason I described it as
a transition was as I said,

00:43:34.070 --> 00:43:36.100
my attempt to mirror
what Bidayuhs themselves

00:43:36.100 --> 00:43:39.660
were describing as a major
change in their lives.

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:42.030
And there's major change was
conversion to Christianity

00:43:42.030 --> 00:43:43.080
as far as they were concerned.

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:44.700
This was huge.

00:43:44.700 --> 00:43:48.600
And it entailed a much more
sort of fundamental overhaul

00:43:48.600 --> 00:43:49.910
of their social relations

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:52.840
and their kind of cosmologies
and religious practices.

00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:57.160
Then some of their other engagements

00:43:57.160 --> 00:43:59.130
with outside forces did.

00:43:59.130 --> 00:44:01.270
For example with missionaries

00:44:01.270 --> 00:44:03.550
who were hanging around in
the area in the 19th century

00:44:03.550 --> 00:44:04.630
and early 20th century

00:44:04.630 --> 00:44:06.910
but who never converted them.

00:44:06.910 --> 00:44:09.490
So in that sense it was
my attempt to also capture

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:12.570
what people saw as a
significant period of change

00:44:12.570 --> 00:44:13.403
leading to the present.

00:44:13.403 --> 00:44:14.900
But I'm absolutely with you.

00:44:14.900 --> 00:44:15.810
They've, you know...

00:44:15.810 --> 00:44:17.400
that in other words, they kind of

00:44:17.400 --> 00:44:20.240
static original romantic past

00:44:20.240 --> 00:44:23.593
they've always engaged with
the world in various ways.

00:44:25.720 --> 00:44:26.980
- Thank you.

00:44:26.980 --> 00:44:28.120
- Thanks, Liana.

00:44:28.120 --> 00:44:29.950
The next question is from Al Lim,

00:44:29.950 --> 00:44:31.320
who's a PhD in student

00:44:31.320 --> 00:44:34.603
in the anthropology
department here at Yale.

00:44:37.350 --> 00:44:39.810
- Hi, Liana, thank you so
much for your presentation.

00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:42.380
It's super fascinating
to think about the ways

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:45.620
people rationalize these
kinds of displacement.

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:47.700
I'm thinking about areas
or to work in Vietnam

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:50.837
and kind of work in the
Macular Hole and the Mitson

00:44:50.837 --> 00:44:52.890
and just across Asia.

00:44:52.890 --> 00:44:55.440
So, but what specific kind
of question about your site,

00:44:55.440 --> 00:44:57.950
you talk about in your article

00:44:57.950 --> 00:45:00.650
Gifting, Dam(n)ing and the
Ambiguation of Development.

00:45:01.832 --> 00:45:03.200
This kind of Christian framework

00:45:03.200 --> 00:45:07.690
that trumps Bidayuh to the
Malaysia development state.

00:45:07.690 --> 00:45:09.390
So I'm wondering this kind of syncretism,

00:45:09.390 --> 00:45:12.860
is it a mode of resistance
or is it a coping mechanism

00:45:12.860 --> 00:45:16.310
and how might this have changed over time?

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:18.060
So you talked about the
kind of planning phase

00:45:18.060 --> 00:45:20.710
of the during phase and the post phase.

00:45:20.710 --> 00:45:21.820
So I'm just wondering how this kind

00:45:21.820 --> 00:45:24.750
of mode of rationalization
changes over time.

00:45:24.750 --> 00:45:26.480
And I know you wrote the
duty and piece at the end

00:45:26.480 --> 00:45:27.800
so I was wondering if you
could speak a little bit

00:45:27.800 --> 00:45:28.633
to that as well.

00:45:28.633 --> 00:45:29.466
Thank you.

00:45:33.270 --> 00:45:34.103
- Okay. Wow.

00:45:34.103 --> 00:45:34.936
Okay. Thank you.

00:45:36.280 --> 00:45:38.000
Yeah, I don't really know

00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:40.330
if it's a sort of resistance
or coping mechanism.

00:45:40.330 --> 00:45:42.250
I mean, in a way I'm trying to refrain.

00:45:42.250 --> 00:45:43.533
I'm doing my own ambiguation in the sense

00:45:43.533 --> 00:45:46.370
that I'm trying to refrain from saying,

00:45:46.370 --> 00:45:48.150
well, this is the explanation,

00:45:48.150 --> 00:45:51.160
this is basically the
function that it's serving.

00:45:51.160 --> 00:45:53.420
I guess the point of that piece

00:45:53.420 --> 00:45:55.440
was to try and reverse the usual way

00:45:55.440 --> 00:45:59.080
that we tend to view sort
of people's engagements

00:45:59.080 --> 00:46:00.100
with development projects,

00:46:00.100 --> 00:46:01.310
rather than trying to use something

00:46:01.310 --> 00:46:04.640
like a sort of for Cody
and governmentality lens

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:08.160
to understand how people
become subjects of development.

00:46:08.160 --> 00:46:09.340
I tried to kind of reverse it and say,

00:46:09.340 --> 00:46:11.040
okay, how does development
become Christianized?

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:12.940
How does it become mythologized?

00:46:12.940 --> 00:46:15.010
Looking through these
sort of indigenous ideas.

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:18.440
And I would consider Christianity
indigenous in its own way.

00:46:18.440 --> 00:46:22.040
It's become a significant
cultural concept.

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:24.960
And I guess if I had to...

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:26.930
If you sort of pin me down,

00:46:26.930 --> 00:46:29.020
I'd probably say that Christianity

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:31.910
is a sort of creative mechanism,
more than anything else.

00:46:31.910 --> 00:46:34.110
It's been an incredibly
sort of powerful means

00:46:34.110 --> 00:46:35.960
through which my interlocutors

00:46:35.960 --> 00:46:39.440
have managed to create
realities for themselves

00:46:39.440 --> 00:46:42.680
in ways that were previously inconceivable

00:46:42.680 --> 00:46:43.970
within the framework of the sort of

00:46:43.970 --> 00:46:45.370
Malaysian developmental state.

00:46:45.370 --> 00:46:47.580
So I've actually currently got an article

00:46:47.580 --> 00:46:50.430
that's just been accepted
in current anthropology.

00:46:50.430 --> 00:46:52.170
And it's looking at...

00:46:52.170 --> 00:46:54.330
It basically looks at the
sort of Christian dimensions

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:56.780
of this legal case that they fought

00:46:56.780 --> 00:47:01.059
and tries to understand how for many of,

00:47:01.059 --> 00:47:03.090
even though the case itself
was sort of fought...

00:47:03.090 --> 00:47:06.570
Was fought in legal, in the
terms of indigenous rights

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:08.390
and sort of legal rights

00:47:08.390 --> 00:47:10.070
behind the scenes it was all about god.

00:47:10.070 --> 00:47:12.290
It was all about them
being good moral Christians

00:47:12.290 --> 00:47:16.010
and trying to enact a sort
of politically engaged

00:47:16.010 --> 00:47:17.290
form of Christianity in the world

00:47:17.290 --> 00:47:19.380
which in fact kind of led them

00:47:19.380 --> 00:47:22.640
to desert the Anglican Church on Merce

00:47:22.640 --> 00:47:26.140
and moved to this West
Malaysian charismatic church

00:47:26.140 --> 00:47:27.320
because they thought the Anglican church

00:47:27.320 --> 00:47:29.650
was just kind of too
tied up with the state.

00:47:29.650 --> 00:47:32.140
And god was a big player in this, right?

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:34.730
So it became a very sort of
powerful productive force

00:47:34.730 --> 00:47:35.990
through which they could actually make

00:47:35.990 --> 00:47:40.030
this new village above the dam a reality.

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:42.420
And as far as they were
concerned, this was god's doing.

00:47:42.420 --> 00:47:43.440
I mean, the lawyers helped

00:47:43.440 --> 00:47:45.950
but this was definitely know
a big cosmological fight

00:47:45.950 --> 00:47:48.300
that they won 'cause they
were good Christians.

00:47:49.220 --> 00:47:53.263
So I guess that's one way of
kind of rethinking development.

00:47:54.232 --> 00:47:55.230
Yeah, the durian piece.

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:57.660
So you're talking about
durian diplomacies?

00:47:57.660 --> 00:47:58.550
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:47:58.550 --> 00:48:00.930
So what's happened, right?

00:48:00.930 --> 00:48:03.447
Just to bring you up to date is that

00:48:03.447 --> 00:48:06.660
these guys eventually won the right to...

00:48:07.560 --> 00:48:08.900
Well, it's a bit tricky.

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:09.733
So they got...

00:48:09.733 --> 00:48:12.670
They forced the state to
recognize their ancestral rights

00:48:12.670 --> 00:48:14.253
to the land above the dam.

00:48:15.820 --> 00:48:16.866
Unfortunately, the state refused,

00:48:16.866 --> 00:48:20.810
still refuses to recognize
their right to have a village

00:48:20.810 --> 00:48:22.610
on that site which was
kind of their point, right?

00:48:22.610 --> 00:48:24.300
So they went and built
this village from scratch

00:48:24.300 --> 00:48:25.970
before they even won the case.

00:48:25.970 --> 00:48:27.370
And they're living there now,

00:48:27.370 --> 00:48:29.630
but because the state
is not supporting it,

00:48:29.630 --> 00:48:33.110
it's refused to give it
subsidies for for pesticides,

00:48:33.110 --> 00:48:37.710
none of the usual solar
panels, money for bamboo,

00:48:37.710 --> 00:48:39.140
water pipe systems, anything.

00:48:39.140 --> 00:48:39.973
They're trying to...

00:48:39.973 --> 00:48:41.750
They become sort of entrepreneurial

00:48:41.750 --> 00:48:43.640
and they're always been entrepreneurial.

00:48:43.640 --> 00:48:45.380
They've always been been selling pepper

00:48:45.380 --> 00:48:48.160
and rubber and cocoa depending
on what the prices are like

00:48:48.160 --> 00:48:50.110
but they're kind of striking
out on their own right now

00:48:50.110 --> 00:48:52.190
which is really, really interesting.

00:48:52.190 --> 00:48:53.490
And one of the things that they've done

00:48:53.490 --> 00:48:55.560
is they've worked with this
(speaks in foreign language)

00:48:55.560 --> 00:48:57.660
So this Chinese businessmen,

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:01.220
who has a durian plantation
growing Musang King

00:49:01.220 --> 00:49:04.180
which like is a sort of
premium brand of durian

00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:07.423
that the China market pays
a huge amount of money for.

00:49:08.520 --> 00:49:10.360
He has this plantion up
in Cameron Highlands.

00:49:10.360 --> 00:49:12.310
And because these guys
were up in the hills

00:49:12.310 --> 00:49:14.500
he's got them to transplant

00:49:14.500 --> 00:49:17.890
some of these Musang King
seedlings over to their land

00:49:17.890 --> 00:49:20.750
and they're growing
these Musang King durians

00:49:20.750 --> 00:49:24.270
and marrying them with the
local variant as they say

00:49:24.270 --> 00:49:27.180
so that they all become
this kind of super hybrid,

00:49:27.180 --> 00:49:28.860
kind of indigenous durians.

00:49:28.860 --> 00:49:33.670
And the hope is that they'll
eventually sell these durians

00:49:33.670 --> 00:49:36.220
to the Chinese market
and make a lot of money.

00:49:36.220 --> 00:49:39.660
So that's a new sort of
strategy that they're using

00:49:39.660 --> 00:49:41.860
to make the most of
their current situation.

00:49:44.930 --> 00:49:46.220
- Thank you, Liana.

00:49:46.220 --> 00:49:49.090
We have a question from Ling Zhang.

00:49:49.090 --> 00:49:51.110
Ling Zhang, do you mind
introducing yourself?

00:49:51.110 --> 00:49:53.810
And also before you ask your question.

00:49:53.810 --> 00:49:54.710
- Sure. Thank you.

00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:57.220
Thank you so much for this wonderful talk.

00:49:57.220 --> 00:49:58.090
Hi Chua.

00:49:59.880 --> 00:50:01.270
I'm not an anthropologist.

00:50:01.270 --> 00:50:04.600
I am actually a historian for China.

00:50:04.600 --> 00:50:07.350
I am working on environmental history.

00:50:07.350 --> 00:50:09.130
So your talk is a fascinating,

00:50:09.130 --> 00:50:14.030
it teaches me a lot about
the ongoing discourses

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:15.860
in your discipline, in your field.

00:50:15.860 --> 00:50:20.360
So I entered this conversation
a few minutes later

00:50:20.360 --> 00:50:22.600
so you might have already
touched upon this.

00:50:22.600 --> 00:50:24.370
I notice in the talk...

00:50:24.370 --> 00:50:29.320
In the title of your
talk there dams there.

00:50:29.320 --> 00:50:31.473
So I might have missed the
conversation about that.

00:50:31.473 --> 00:50:34.310
Can you say something about dams?

00:50:34.310 --> 00:50:36.240
And also I noticed in your talk

00:50:36.240 --> 00:50:38.420
and in several of your publication,

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:41.270
you mentioned troubled landscapes.

00:50:41.270 --> 00:50:43.520
So could you please also say something

00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:45.260
about the troubled landscapes.

00:50:45.260 --> 00:50:46.820
So what do you mean?

00:50:46.820 --> 00:50:51.120
And related to dams and
the troubled landscape

00:50:51.120 --> 00:50:53.960
but then this lead to another issue.

00:50:53.960 --> 00:50:58.300
I'm very curious about
that something here.

00:50:59.770 --> 00:51:02.050
What is the...

00:51:03.190 --> 00:51:06.610
How do anthropologists like you

00:51:06.610 --> 00:51:10.770
working on more than human
and a multi-species issue

00:51:10.770 --> 00:51:15.290
to incorporate non-life forms?

00:51:15.290 --> 00:51:19.820
Let's say dance, let's say
gigantic concrete monsters

00:51:19.820 --> 00:51:23.010
non-organic beings.

00:51:23.010 --> 00:51:24.830
How do you deal with that?

00:51:24.830 --> 00:51:28.280
Not necessarily life-based.

00:51:28.280 --> 00:51:31.260
Not necessarily species-based.

00:51:31.260 --> 00:51:32.940
So I'm just purely curious

00:51:32.940 --> 00:51:36.380
about what's the conversation
in your field about that.

00:51:36.380 --> 00:51:37.330
Thank you.

00:51:37.330 --> 00:51:38.740
- Yeah. Well, thank you so much.

00:51:38.740 --> 00:51:41.530
Yeah. I think they're sort of...

00:51:41.530 --> 00:51:43.390
They're all very much connected.

00:51:43.390 --> 00:51:45.050
Yeah, I mean, in some ways,

00:51:45.050 --> 00:51:48.040
it feels like multi-species
as a kind of approach,

00:51:48.040 --> 00:51:50.190
isn't necessarily expensive enough

00:51:50.190 --> 00:51:52.817
to incorporate everything
that we wanna talk about.

00:51:52.817 --> 00:51:55.890
And so that's why I use
the term more than human

00:51:55.890 --> 00:51:57.010
rather than multi-species

00:51:57.010 --> 00:51:59.700
because a lot of my work has been,

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:03.320
has involved trying to
understand this incredibly

00:52:03.320 --> 00:52:08.170
sort of powerful and really
quite problematic agency,

00:52:08.170 --> 00:52:09.003
I guess

00:52:09.003 --> 00:52:12.680
that these non-living beings
can have on people's lives

00:52:12.680 --> 00:52:15.480
and how they kind of co-create
these sorts of realities.

00:52:16.598 --> 00:52:17.480
And so this kind of brings me

00:52:17.480 --> 00:52:19.520
to the notion of dams
and Troubled landscapes.

00:52:19.520 --> 00:52:22.550
So these are basically
connected in the sense that

00:52:23.390 --> 00:52:25.520
my paper, Troubled
landscapes was very much

00:52:25.520 --> 00:52:30.400
an attempt to understand how effectively

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:33.560
my interlocutors were becoming displaced

00:52:33.560 --> 00:52:35.480
from their own landscape,

00:52:35.480 --> 00:52:40.480
their own kind of sort of
genealogical, social, moral space,

00:52:42.100 --> 00:52:43.180
which they call (speaks
in foreign language)

00:52:43.180 --> 00:52:47.240
which means basically our area
without ever having left it.

00:52:47.240 --> 00:52:48.570
And this was...

00:52:48.570 --> 00:52:51.189
That was where the sort of
troubledness came in, right?

00:52:51.189 --> 00:52:53.910
That they were still living in this space

00:52:53.910 --> 00:52:55.950
that was undergoing these
really quite radical

00:52:55.950 --> 00:53:00.490
and far reaching physical changes
that then had implications

00:53:00.490 --> 00:53:02.050
for the way they related to the land.

00:53:02.050 --> 00:53:06.590
So in this article I wrote about how prior

00:53:07.971 --> 00:53:09.742
to the dam coming along,

00:53:09.742 --> 00:53:14.490
they sort of use things like
fruit trees and landmarks

00:53:14.490 --> 00:53:19.490
and kind of bamboo, rows of
bamboo, old long house posts

00:53:20.040 --> 00:53:24.717
as sort of indexes of their
genealogies and their movements

00:53:24.717 --> 00:53:27.910
in the sort of migration
patterns across that land

00:53:27.910 --> 00:53:31.230
which could in turn be ways of
understanding people's rights

00:53:31.230 --> 00:53:34.080
and relationships and kinship
obligations in the present.

00:53:35.700 --> 00:53:37.980
And of course, once the dam came along

00:53:37.980 --> 00:53:41.270
and the landscape began
being kind of reshaped

00:53:41.270 --> 00:53:43.660
in anticipation of inundation,

00:53:43.660 --> 00:53:46.530
all of that started to
disappear really quickly.

00:53:46.530 --> 00:53:51.530
So you didn't just have
this sudden dam landscape

00:53:51.640 --> 00:53:54.200
that was opening up in front of them,

00:53:54.200 --> 00:53:55.230
which they had to walk through.

00:53:55.230 --> 00:53:56.063
It took...

00:53:56.063 --> 00:53:57.290
I mean, it would take
about an hour sometimes

00:53:57.290 --> 00:53:58.420
when the weather was bad to get

00:53:58.420 --> 00:54:00.600
through this construction site.

00:54:00.600 --> 00:54:01.910
And it was absolutely terrifying.

00:54:01.910 --> 00:54:04.500
You'd have boulders kind of
falling down occasionally,

00:54:04.500 --> 00:54:05.970
you'd have these burst water pipes,

00:54:05.970 --> 00:54:08.300
you'd have these huge machines

00:54:08.300 --> 00:54:10.710
that you'd have to somehow
pick your way around.

00:54:10.710 --> 00:54:13.000
And people would always
talk about these things

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:16.890
as if they had a kind of malign
agency and force themselves.

00:54:16.890 --> 00:54:19.480
Oh, so and so, one of
those workers fell off

00:54:19.480 --> 00:54:21.690
that water tower or whatever recently

00:54:21.690 --> 00:54:23.280
broke open his head, they buried him.

00:54:23.280 --> 00:54:24.810
They didn't know where he came from.

00:54:24.810 --> 00:54:26.770
You get all these stories
as you were kind of walking

00:54:26.770 --> 00:54:30.300
through the dam construction site.

00:54:30.300 --> 00:54:33.030
So I guess in that sense,

00:54:33.030 --> 00:54:35.680
what was happening was
this really weird...

00:54:35.680 --> 00:54:38.360
This weird sort of process of displacement

00:54:38.360 --> 00:54:42.630
that didn't involve necessarily
a kind of relocation

00:54:42.630 --> 00:54:45.030
or migration to a different site,

00:54:45.030 --> 00:54:45.900
but that was nevertheless

00:54:45.900 --> 00:54:49.320
completely reshaping people's lives.

00:54:49.320 --> 00:54:50.520
So that's kind of what I meant.

00:54:50.520 --> 00:54:53.560
And yeah, I mean, absolutely
the dam was a huge thing.

00:54:53.560 --> 00:54:56.210
Not just the sort of big
physical cement structure

00:54:56.210 --> 00:54:59.440
which is big, but also it's spectra.

00:54:59.440 --> 00:55:03.750
It's possibility, which change things

00:55:03.750 --> 00:55:07.070
and people's relations way
before the waters ever came.

00:55:07.070 --> 00:55:08.620
And then when the waters
came, it was all about

00:55:08.620 --> 00:55:10.960
how they was stinky and deadly and toxic.

00:55:10.960 --> 00:55:13.960
And I mean, yeah, there were
many, many sort of affective

00:55:13.960 --> 00:55:15.930
and very visceral associations

00:55:15.930 --> 00:55:19.430
produced by these non-human
things including dams.

00:55:19.430 --> 00:55:21.444
So, yeah, I don't think
multi-species is necessarily

00:55:21.444 --> 00:55:24.710
a big enough term to
capture what was going on.

00:55:24.710 --> 00:55:27.020
- Right, so in the sense
of the Troubled landscapes

00:55:27.020 --> 00:55:30.970
creates a many new spirits
too in this new era?

00:55:30.970 --> 00:55:31.803
Sounds like.

00:55:31.803 --> 00:55:33.940
- Yeah, I think so. I think so.

00:55:33.940 --> 00:55:35.540
I think we're still working that one out.

00:55:35.540 --> 00:55:38.010
I mean, I know it upset many old spirits

00:55:38.010 --> 00:55:40.340
so there were hauntings in the dormitory

00:55:40.340 --> 00:55:42.230
that we heard about a lot,

00:55:42.230 --> 00:55:44.029
it was all about the old
waterways getting blocked up

00:55:44.029 --> 00:55:46.920
and spirits getting really off about that.

00:55:46.920 --> 00:55:48.380
Yeah. There was all that going on as well.

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:49.610
There might...

00:55:49.610 --> 00:55:51.260
I think they said there might be hauntings

00:55:51.260 --> 00:55:53.390
by the spirits of the workers who died

00:55:53.390 --> 00:55:55.720
but I haven't had anything since then.

00:55:55.720 --> 00:55:57.820
So I don't know if that's still-

00:55:57.820 --> 00:55:58.950
- Thank you so much.

00:55:58.950 --> 00:55:59.783
Thank you.

00:56:01.540 --> 00:56:03.960
- I believe we have a
question from James Scott

00:56:03.960 --> 00:56:07.640
and then after Jim we have
questions from Ayesha Kashani,

00:56:07.640 --> 00:56:10.150
Shoko Yamada and Kristin S.

00:56:10.150 --> 00:56:12.550
And we have about 20 minutes left.

00:56:12.550 --> 00:56:13.599
It's one o'clock for those of you

00:56:13.599 --> 00:56:15.840
who have to leave at one o'clock sometime

00:56:15.840 --> 00:56:16.770
but we have...

00:56:17.700 --> 00:56:19.150
We can stick around a little longer.

00:56:19.150 --> 00:56:20.840
So I'll let James take the question

00:56:20.840 --> 00:56:22.290
and then we'll turn to the others.

00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:23.650
Go ahead, James.

00:56:23.650 --> 00:56:24.483
- Thanks a lot.

00:56:24.483 --> 00:56:28.173
It was a wonderful talk and provocative.

00:56:29.330 --> 00:56:32.970
What I wanted to ask
you to speculate about

00:56:33.820 --> 00:56:37.977
is the relationship between monotheism

00:56:39.930 --> 00:56:44.542
as an anthropological
reality on the ground

00:56:44.542 --> 00:56:48.420
not in terms of its books and doctrine,

00:56:48.420 --> 00:56:50.880
but Islam or Christianity

00:56:50.880 --> 00:56:54.280
especially the hard edge
diversion that's recently come

00:56:55.350 --> 00:56:59.630
and its relationship to the
non-human world generally.

00:56:59.630 --> 00:57:00.760
I mean, one of the,

00:57:00.760 --> 00:57:03.030
from an abstract perspective,

00:57:03.030 --> 00:57:05.830
one of the attractions I think

00:57:05.830 --> 00:57:10.830
to environmentalist of
animism is the presumption

00:57:11.090 --> 00:57:16.090
that the non-human world
has agency spirit and so on

00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:22.020
and therefore demands a
certain kind of respect.

00:57:22.020 --> 00:57:25.550
I know Pfizer Zachary
was a colleague of yours

00:57:25.550 --> 00:57:27.293
in history at NUS.

00:57:27.293 --> 00:57:30.433
He's working on these issues in Sumatra.

00:57:31.320 --> 00:57:35.867
But I'd like you to say
something about monotheism

00:57:36.880 --> 00:57:40.940
on the ground as it's
understood and practiced

00:57:40.940 --> 00:57:45.580
and whether it's relationship
to the non-human world

00:57:45.580 --> 00:57:49.720
is different, radically
different, moderately different

00:57:49.720 --> 00:57:54.720
from let's say the older
spirit loving practitioners

00:57:58.000 --> 00:57:59.243
at your field site.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:02.811
- Interesting, thank you very much

00:58:02.811 --> 00:58:04.733
for putting that thought in my head.

00:58:08.500 --> 00:58:11.430
It's hard for me to say whether or not

00:58:12.562 --> 00:58:14.650
the Christians I'm familiar with

00:58:14.650 --> 00:58:17.390
have a particularly strong
concept of monotheism.

00:58:17.390 --> 00:58:19.360
I think they're sort of...

00:58:19.360 --> 00:58:21.220
They're very aware that
the Christian god...

00:58:21.220 --> 00:58:22.199
So I'm really speaking

00:58:22.199 --> 00:58:24.090
from this underground experience here.

00:58:24.090 --> 00:58:28.340
They're very aware that the
Christian god is the most...

00:58:28.340 --> 00:58:30.040
Is the one true god

00:58:30.040 --> 00:58:32.713
but that doesn't necessarily
make him the only god.

00:58:34.050 --> 00:58:36.613
So I think as far as they're concerned

00:58:36.613 --> 00:58:37.480
that the sort of...

00:58:37.480 --> 00:58:40.530
One of the biggest
attractions of Christianity

00:58:40.530 --> 00:58:42.230
when people initially converted

00:58:42.230 --> 00:58:43.870
was that it was seen to free.

00:58:43.870 --> 00:58:46.230
And they used the term bia, which means...

00:58:46.230 --> 00:58:47.340
Which is a ritual term.

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:51.460
It means free from a ritual
taboo to free Bidayuhs

00:58:51.460 --> 00:58:55.270
from the sort of
obligations and difficulties

00:58:55.270 --> 00:58:57.070
and challenges and hardships

00:58:57.070 --> 00:58:59.413
that were caused by
the old animus rituals.

00:59:00.326 --> 00:59:01.630
Now, it's very hard to tell

00:59:01.630 --> 00:59:03.750
whether this is a kind
of retrospective gloss

00:59:03.750 --> 00:59:05.930
that people put on the old animus rituals

00:59:05.930 --> 00:59:07.103
as a way of kind of
justifying their conversion

00:59:07.103 --> 00:59:08.130
to Christianity.

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:10.280
But the sort of standard
story that you hear is

00:59:10.280 --> 00:59:12.900
in the olden days things were really hard

00:59:12.900 --> 00:59:15.330
because the spirits were
really unpredictable.

00:59:15.330 --> 00:59:16.830
There were lots of (speaks
in foreign language)

00:59:16.830 --> 00:59:19.640
lots of taboos that you might come across.

00:59:19.640 --> 00:59:21.870
If you were walking to farm

00:59:21.870 --> 00:59:24.210
and you had a busy day's
work harvesting the rice,

00:59:24.210 --> 00:59:26.250
if you saw a bird flying
cost you three times

00:59:26.250 --> 00:59:27.090
in a certain direction,

00:59:27.090 --> 00:59:28.510
oh no you'd have to stop immediately

00:59:28.510 --> 00:59:29.970
because that was an omen

00:59:29.970 --> 00:59:32.440
and then you'd be punished
if you didn't obey it.

00:59:32.440 --> 00:59:33.620
And so people often talked about

00:59:33.620 --> 00:59:34.770
these very sort of unpredictable

00:59:34.770 --> 00:59:38.430
but also very kind of
time-consuming restrictions

00:59:38.430 --> 00:59:40.840
that they had to live with in the old days

00:59:40.840 --> 00:59:43.460
as one of the main reasons that they chose

00:59:43.460 --> 00:59:45.860
to convert to Christianity.

00:59:45.860 --> 00:59:47.140
They were free.

00:59:47.140 --> 00:59:49.120
And the reason they were free was not that

00:59:49.120 --> 00:59:51.820
they didn't believe the old
spirits existed anymore.

00:59:51.820 --> 00:59:54.330
It was that they realized
that the old spirits

00:59:54.330 --> 00:59:56.920
were now being trumped
by more powerful spirit

00:59:56.920 --> 00:59:59.370
which was god or Jesus
or Mary or whatever.

00:59:59.370 --> 01:00:00.350
And they had weapons.

01:00:00.350 --> 01:00:04.850
They had crucifixes,
altars, holy water and so on

01:00:04.850 --> 01:00:08.130
with which they could kind of
combat any malign influences

01:00:08.130 --> 01:00:10.660
whether it was Satan or
whether it was the old spirits

01:00:10.660 --> 01:00:12.620
that were causing trouble for them.

01:00:12.620 --> 01:00:15.020
So, I mean, it's hard to say whether

01:00:15.020 --> 01:00:18.470
that is a form of
monotheism on the ground,

01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:22.740
I suppose in the sense that theoretically

01:00:22.740 --> 01:00:26.350
I think everyone subscribes
to a relatively purified model

01:00:26.350 --> 01:00:27.720
of Christianity.

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:29.900
They will say the right things

01:00:29.900 --> 01:00:32.160
and they know in theory what the story,

01:00:32.160 --> 01:00:35.060
they call it stories is meant to be about.

01:00:35.060 --> 01:00:38.160
In practice, I think it's
a much more difficult

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:41.700
sort of juggling act between
the sort of realities

01:00:41.700 --> 01:00:45.100
of the Christian present
and the continued,

01:00:45.100 --> 01:00:48.150
the persistent remnants of
the pre-Christian past that

01:00:49.320 --> 01:00:50.306
I mean, they're just there.

01:00:50.306 --> 01:00:53.590
They're not even possibilities

01:00:53.590 --> 01:00:56.170
they're just there in people's lives.

01:00:56.170 --> 01:00:59.290
And so I guess sort of relating this point

01:00:59.290 --> 01:01:03.393
to the non-human world, I think
maybe one of of the things

01:01:03.393 --> 01:01:06.360
that I find environmentalist
tend to get wrong

01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:10.080
is kind of this assumption
that all indigenous people

01:01:10.080 --> 01:01:14.160
love and care about and have
kin relations with and so on

01:01:17.110 --> 01:01:18.223
non-human beings.

01:01:20.430 --> 01:01:22.540
I don't think that's necessarily true.

01:01:22.540 --> 01:01:23.590
I mean, when you sort of look at

01:01:23.590 --> 01:01:25.090
these sorts of relations on the ground,

01:01:25.090 --> 01:01:27.730
we find that people
would just as interested

01:01:27.730 --> 01:01:32.420
in drawing boundaries
and very often people

01:01:32.420 --> 01:01:35.150
would have kind of antagonistic relations

01:01:35.150 --> 01:01:37.110
with certain beings and certain animals,

01:01:37.110 --> 01:01:40.363
certain parts of nature,
however you define that.

01:01:42.080 --> 01:01:44.980
And so I'm not sure
necessarily if Christianity

01:01:44.980 --> 01:01:48.490
might've inacted a change
so much as that stereotype

01:01:48.490 --> 01:01:53.490
about indigenous people
may not be all that true.

01:01:55.740 --> 01:01:57.450
I'm generalizing a little bit here,

01:01:57.450 --> 01:02:02.450
but certainly from what I've
seen in the Borneo context

01:02:04.877 --> 01:02:05.710
that's not the case,

01:02:05.710 --> 01:02:09.290
there's a much more kind of
complex range of emotions

01:02:09.290 --> 01:02:11.340
and relations and boundaries that you see

01:02:11.340 --> 01:02:13.640
in people's relations
with non-human spirits

01:02:13.640 --> 01:02:15.093
and non-humans animals.

01:02:18.610 --> 01:02:20.850
- Great, we'll move to the next question

01:02:20.850 --> 01:02:23.870
from Ayesha Kashani.

01:02:23.870 --> 01:02:24.703
- Hi, Liana.

01:02:24.703 --> 01:02:27.130
Thank you for the time.
- [Liana] Hi.

01:02:27.130 --> 01:02:27.980
Yeah, I mean, okay.

01:02:27.980 --> 01:02:29.190
So I'm a PhD student

01:02:29.190 --> 01:02:31.660
at Goldsmiths University of
London in visual cultures

01:02:31.660 --> 01:02:34.270
and my work is looking at cosmic
politics and the histories

01:02:34.270 --> 01:02:36.943
and futures of exhibiting
nature at the Sorel Museum.

01:02:38.099 --> 01:02:39.320
And so I just wanted to ask,

01:02:39.320 --> 01:02:41.670
I mean about your reference
to cosmic politics.

01:02:42.540 --> 01:02:43.760
If you can speak a little bit

01:02:43.760 --> 01:02:45.410
to the methodologies and practices

01:02:45.410 --> 01:02:47.360
that you're working with
in terms of mediating

01:02:47.360 --> 01:02:49.800
between conservationists and communities

01:02:49.800 --> 01:02:52.830
and orangutans and the
state and everything else,

01:02:52.830 --> 01:02:55.760
and how if you all using a kind

01:02:55.760 --> 01:02:57.130
of cosmic political framework

01:02:57.130 --> 01:02:59.090
in terms of negotiating between those

01:02:59.090 --> 01:03:00.357
and how it's working out.

01:03:01.800 --> 01:03:05.873
- Yeah, I think I use
it sort of selectively.

01:03:06.850 --> 01:03:09.840
So, I mean, they're obviously
sort of different approaches

01:03:09.840 --> 01:03:11.140
to cosmos politics.

01:03:11.140 --> 01:03:13.710
The one that I'm finding
most useful at this point

01:03:13.710 --> 01:03:16.610
is the one that's used
by Marisol de la Cadena

01:03:16.610 --> 01:03:19.200
and her colleague, Mario Blaser

01:03:20.070 --> 01:03:21.680
in relationship to sort
of indigenous groups

01:03:21.680 --> 01:03:23.336
in North and South America.

01:03:23.336 --> 01:03:25.320
And what these are sort of built around

01:03:25.320 --> 01:03:27.980
are kind of ontological tarnish desire

01:03:27.980 --> 01:03:30.560
to try and take their spirit beliefs,

01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:32.700
their relations with things like

01:03:32.700 --> 01:03:35.290
mountains, important
mountains, other spirits,

01:03:35.290 --> 01:03:39.990
again, on ontological
turns and to actually

01:03:39.990 --> 01:03:42.710
rather than kind of explaining
them away as just beliefs

01:03:42.710 --> 01:03:46.060
or kind of cultural
glosses or superstitions,

01:03:46.060 --> 01:03:48.140
trying to understand the
sort of political role

01:03:48.140 --> 01:03:50.810
that these non-human entities can play

01:03:50.810 --> 01:03:53.930
in their politics and their
relations with the state.

01:03:53.930 --> 01:03:55.630
And I think the sort of
main thing that I take away

01:03:55.630 --> 01:03:57.488
from this particular body of work is that

01:03:57.488 --> 01:04:00.410
it advocates an approach to difference

01:04:00.410 --> 01:04:03.600
and incommensurability quite specifically

01:04:05.420 --> 01:04:08.480
that does not go for...

01:04:09.568 --> 01:04:11.670
That does not go for a kind of hybrid

01:04:11.670 --> 01:04:13.570
or neutralizing end, right?

01:04:13.570 --> 01:04:16.390
So if you get, say four
different kind of increments,

01:04:16.390 --> 01:04:21.390
rubble, complexes,
practices, people together,

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:24.170
what they sort of
advocate as a way for them

01:04:24.170 --> 01:04:27.970
to kind of come together
through partial connections.

01:04:27.970 --> 01:04:29.320
So this is what de la Cadena

01:04:29.320 --> 01:04:32.650
drawing on (indistinct) uses,

01:04:32.650 --> 01:04:36.780
but without allowing any
party to encompass or dominate

01:04:36.780 --> 01:04:41.780
or run over the other such
that that original difference

01:04:42.340 --> 01:04:44.880
is preserved, is maintained.

01:04:44.880 --> 01:04:46.660
So it's a kind of...

01:04:46.660 --> 01:04:50.350
It's a sort of alignment and
a way forward momentarily

01:04:50.350 --> 01:04:52.500
without nullifying the
original difference.

01:04:54.160 --> 01:04:57.150
And I think that is actually
a very useful approach

01:04:57.150 --> 01:04:58.653
in various ways to,

01:04:59.800 --> 01:05:01.620
particularly to orangutan conservation

01:05:01.620 --> 01:05:04.710
where you've constantly got
conservationists and the state

01:05:04.710 --> 01:05:06.160
trying to change mindsets.

01:05:06.160 --> 01:05:09.560
This is a very popular phrase
in orangutan conservation.

01:05:09.560 --> 01:05:10.470
You know, it's always like

01:05:10.470 --> 01:05:12.250
how do we change these villages mindsets?

01:05:12.250 --> 01:05:14.410
How do we change these
indigenous people's mindsets

01:05:14.410 --> 01:05:16.931
and get them to love
orangutans, for example.

01:05:16.931 --> 01:05:18.790
And I guess one of the
things I've been trying

01:05:18.790 --> 01:05:21.180
to encourage conservationists
to think about

01:05:21.180 --> 01:05:22.040
is how they can adopt

01:05:22.040 --> 01:05:24.428
a slightly more cosmic
political approach to this

01:05:24.428 --> 01:05:28.400
rather than trying to
completely change the way

01:05:28.400 --> 01:05:30.220
these were all going to
just think about orangutans,

01:05:30.220 --> 01:05:31.143
for example.

01:05:32.248 --> 01:05:34.019
Can you sort of find ways through

01:05:34.019 --> 01:05:37.360
that don't involve forcing them to adapt

01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:41.480
to your particular values or
your particular assumptions

01:05:41.480 --> 01:05:44.100
about sort of human-animal relations,

01:05:44.100 --> 01:05:46.263
about about how special orangutans are?

01:05:47.580 --> 01:05:50.050
And actually what I might
do is very quickly respond

01:05:50.050 --> 01:05:55.050
to Michael's comment in the
texts because this is relevant.

01:05:56.200 --> 01:05:57.548
This was...

01:05:57.548 --> 01:06:00.067
The orangutan was kind of tabooed

01:06:01.720 --> 01:06:02.970
in certain parts of Borneo.

01:06:02.970 --> 01:06:07.220
But as far as I know, it was
not across the entire Island.

01:06:07.220 --> 01:06:11.260
So I do know that there have
been various 19th century,

01:06:11.260 --> 01:06:14.920
early 20th century sources
and some contemporary stories

01:06:14.920 --> 01:06:18.200
particularly among various
Iban communities as you say

01:06:18.200 --> 01:06:20.550
about how it has a special relationship

01:06:20.550 --> 01:06:22.860
to contemporary villages

01:06:22.860 --> 01:06:24.560
particularly in the way it was set

01:06:26.307 --> 01:06:29.520
to teach humans how to give birth safely.

01:06:29.520 --> 01:06:31.260
So the idea was that there's an Iban guy

01:06:31.260 --> 01:06:33.340
who went out to the forest,

01:06:33.340 --> 01:06:36.730
saw an orangutan mother
being helped to give birth

01:06:36.730 --> 01:06:38.050
by other children.

01:06:38.050 --> 01:06:41.500
And that persuaded the Iban
men to stop the old practice

01:06:41.500 --> 01:06:43.190
of apparently cutting
open women's stomachs

01:06:43.190 --> 01:06:45.220
when they gave birth, which
was probably quite bad

01:06:45.220 --> 01:06:47.397
and adopt this method of
helping to massage out

01:06:47.397 --> 01:06:48.620
and the child.

01:06:48.620 --> 01:06:50.730
So there are certain villages

01:06:50.730 --> 01:06:53.630
in which historically the
orangutan was a special

01:06:53.630 --> 01:06:54.813
and taboo creature,

01:06:56.322 --> 01:07:01.322
but that's not as far as I can
tell widespread across Borneo

01:07:02.390 --> 01:07:03.700
and it's not called it...

01:07:03.700 --> 01:07:05.830
It's not necessarily different

01:07:05.830 --> 01:07:09.780
to any other villages special
relation with other animals.

01:07:09.780 --> 01:07:10.880
So it may not be an orangutan.

01:07:10.880 --> 01:07:12.691
It might be a snake or a crocodile

01:07:12.691 --> 01:07:15.353
or a particular bird or something else.

01:07:16.401 --> 01:07:19.220
And again to go back to Ayesha's point

01:07:19.220 --> 01:07:21.820
this is where it is quite
important to try and avoid

01:07:23.240 --> 01:07:25.750
sort of stereotypical or generalized views

01:07:25.750 --> 01:07:27.320
about the kind of difference

01:07:27.320 --> 01:07:30.030
that you're trying to
overcome as a conservationist.

01:07:30.030 --> 01:07:31.150
There are many conservationists

01:07:31.150 --> 01:07:33.610
who jumped on this idea of the orangutan

01:07:33.610 --> 01:07:36.190
as a tabooed animal was a special animal

01:07:36.190 --> 01:07:39.230
to very, very specific
communities, Iban communities

01:07:39.230 --> 01:07:40.063
in the past.

01:07:40.063 --> 01:07:42.070
And they say, well we wanna help them

01:07:42.070 --> 01:07:45.040
remember their old rituals.

01:07:45.040 --> 01:07:45.883
Let's do it.

01:07:46.890 --> 01:07:47.830
And I guess my question is,

01:07:47.830 --> 01:07:49.950
is there sort of a
better way of doing this

01:07:49.950 --> 01:07:54.040
that somehow allows them sort
of respects that difference

01:07:54.040 --> 01:07:57.010
and allows them to sort
of find a way through

01:07:57.010 --> 01:07:59.993
without trying to nullify
their distinctiveness.

01:08:02.081 --> 01:08:04.670
- I see that Jim wanted to jump in.

01:08:04.670 --> 01:08:06.370
Jim, you had your hand up really quick.

01:08:06.370 --> 01:08:07.203
Is it...

01:08:07.203 --> 01:08:08.910
Did you have something
you wanted to jump in

01:08:08.910 --> 01:08:12.223
or can I jump to Shoko and Kristin?

01:08:14.300 --> 01:08:17.210
Okay, Shoko Yamada who is a PhD student

01:08:17.210 --> 01:08:20.440
in the anthropology and school
of environment here at Yale

01:08:20.440 --> 01:08:21.273
has a question.

01:08:22.580 --> 01:08:24.130
- Hi, thank you so much for this talk.

01:08:24.130 --> 01:08:26.910
I was fascinating constantly throughout

01:08:26.910 --> 01:08:29.490
and I especially love example
you were talking about

01:08:29.490 --> 01:08:33.170
in terms of the heterogeneity of ontology

01:08:33.170 --> 01:08:35.730
how there's like not a single ontology

01:08:35.730 --> 01:08:38.200
that you could talk about on the ground.

01:08:38.200 --> 01:08:39.033
And I was thinking about

01:08:39.033 --> 01:08:41.910
in relation to maybe
Michael's earlier question

01:08:41.910 --> 01:08:45.350
about transition and
Borneo being in transition

01:08:45.350 --> 01:08:47.560
and the fact that when
you are doing field work

01:08:47.560 --> 01:08:50.040
a lot of people are
going through conversion

01:08:50.040 --> 01:08:52.370
to Christianity or relatively recently

01:08:52.370 --> 01:08:54.950
and going through this change.

01:08:54.950 --> 01:08:58.410
And I was thinking about how
maybe we could think about

01:08:58.410 --> 01:09:02.920
that as like another form of
heterogeneity almost across,

01:09:02.920 --> 01:09:05.770
not in terms of like space
where like lots of people

01:09:05.770 --> 01:09:08.156
are disagreeing about stuff
and debating about things

01:09:08.156 --> 01:09:10.690
that kind of a heterogeneity across time

01:09:10.690 --> 01:09:15.690
within the supposedly same single self.

01:09:16.180 --> 01:09:17.020
So I was...

01:09:17.020 --> 01:09:19.610
I guess I have two
related questions on this.

01:09:19.610 --> 01:09:22.660
One is just on the ground
how people are living

01:09:22.660 --> 01:09:24.790
through and grappling with
this kind of transition

01:09:24.790 --> 01:09:28.930
or maybe like whether you, or I just hope,

01:09:28.930 --> 01:09:31.230
like with our there's a
sense of contradiction,

01:09:31.230 --> 01:09:34.360
is there a sense of nostalgia tars,

01:09:34.360 --> 01:09:36.990
a different kind of paths,
or is it seen as progress?

01:09:36.990 --> 01:09:39.710
I'm just kind of curious
how ethnographically

01:09:39.710 --> 01:09:41.000
how people are dealing with this.

01:09:41.000 --> 01:09:43.860
And the second one is I
guess is a little more

01:09:44.950 --> 01:09:47.590
the theoretical one in
terms of your critique

01:09:47.590 --> 01:09:51.220
of the ontological turn and how

01:09:52.420 --> 01:09:55.040
and kind of I guess the question
about how the parent might

01:09:55.040 --> 01:09:56.230
or might not be able to deal

01:09:56.230 --> 01:09:59.900
with this kind of
historical change over time.

01:09:59.900 --> 01:10:02.040
And yeah, so I was kind of wondering

01:10:02.040 --> 01:10:03.160
if you could speak about that

01:10:03.160 --> 01:10:06.460
and how you are a guest suggestion to deal

01:10:06.460 --> 01:10:09.560
with more of the co-presence
between the ethnography person

01:10:09.560 --> 01:10:10.490
and the interlocutors

01:10:10.490 --> 01:10:13.660
as opposed to kind of the
radical alternative might

01:10:13.660 --> 01:10:16.160
or might not help us kind of deal

01:10:16.160 --> 01:10:19.290
with this kind of change
that the term might

01:10:19.290 --> 01:10:21.300
or might not miss.

01:10:21.300 --> 01:10:23.920
Yeah, those are it's a lot, but yeah.

01:10:23.920 --> 01:10:27.093
If you could speak any speak
to any aspect with them.

01:10:27.093 --> 01:10:28.242
- Yeah. Thank you very much.

01:10:28.242 --> 01:10:29.075
I mean, that's...

01:10:29.075 --> 01:10:31.240
Yeah, that's a really nice
way of articulating it.

01:10:31.240 --> 01:10:32.450
Thank you.

01:10:32.450 --> 01:10:35.100
Yeah. Heterogeneity across time.

01:10:35.100 --> 01:10:36.613
That's a really good point.

01:10:37.460 --> 01:10:39.050
And I mean, there've been
sort of various critiques

01:10:39.050 --> 01:10:41.810
of the ontological turn
that kind of also point out

01:10:41.810 --> 01:10:44.770
how that they're not
necessarily very helpfully,

01:10:44.770 --> 01:10:46.650
constantly dealing with the ways

01:10:46.650 --> 01:10:50.150
in which people's views
or approaches or selves

01:10:50.150 --> 01:10:51.600
and relations might change over time.

01:10:51.600 --> 01:10:53.860
So I think you're absolutely right.

01:10:53.860 --> 01:10:56.580
How would people living
through transition?

01:10:56.580 --> 01:11:01.580
Yeah, one way which I
find really interesting

01:11:01.690 --> 01:11:05.050
and which also in some
ways kind of broaden

01:11:05.050 --> 01:11:09.210
my kind of anti-colonial
sensibilities I guess.

01:11:09.210 --> 01:11:10.960
One thing I found really
interesting is that

01:11:10.960 --> 01:11:15.960
there's been a kind of
romanticization of the British past.

01:11:20.330 --> 01:11:24.770
Zimmerman British, German,
German as they say.

01:11:24.770 --> 01:11:27.770
And by British, Bidayuhs tend to mean

01:11:27.770 --> 01:11:29.400
both the British colonial government

01:11:29.400 --> 01:11:32.820
and the Brooke Raj,
which was a curious beast

01:11:32.820 --> 01:11:37.130
that roots row up in
the century before that.

01:11:37.130 --> 01:11:41.220
And what is interesting here is that

01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:44.260
there is a kind of nostalgia developing

01:11:44.260 --> 01:11:48.110
around how in the olden days,

01:11:48.110 --> 01:11:51.992
the British would care for

01:11:51.992 --> 01:11:54.568
where they were kindly
women, this kind generous,

01:11:54.568 --> 01:11:57.760
they would care for people like themselves

01:11:57.760 --> 01:11:59.900
because they wouldn't
eat their land, right?

01:11:59.900 --> 01:12:01.090
This is a term they use.

01:12:01.090 --> 01:12:01.923
They don't...

01:12:01.923 --> 01:12:03.360
The British did not eat our land.

01:12:03.360 --> 01:12:04.610
And they gave us some development.

01:12:04.610 --> 01:12:07.720
They gave us rubber plantations,
they gave us water tanks.

01:12:07.720 --> 01:12:09.970
They brought Christianity
who brought education

01:12:09.970 --> 01:12:12.230
and medicines.

01:12:12.230 --> 01:12:16.670
And this is then used
as a kind of moral foil

01:12:16.670 --> 01:12:19.190
to the contemporary Malaysian government

01:12:19.190 --> 01:12:22.750
which they see as only ever
wanting to eat their lands.

01:12:22.750 --> 01:12:25.280
And the fear of land
grabs is quite sort of

01:12:25.280 --> 01:12:26.840
justified in Sarawak

01:12:27.810 --> 01:12:29.470
and also withholding development from them

01:12:29.470 --> 01:12:31.760
because they're not Muslim.

01:12:31.760 --> 01:12:34.810
And so there's a really
interesting kind of...

01:12:34.810 --> 01:12:36.180
It's almost like a sort of donate to cue

01:12:36.180 --> 01:12:37.853
where you've kind of got

01:12:37.853 --> 01:12:40.600
this romanticized Brooke British past

01:12:40.600 --> 01:12:44.860
set against what they say is a
very, very unpleasant reality

01:12:44.860 --> 01:12:47.490
of sort of Malaysian present.

01:12:47.490 --> 01:12:50.040
And I suppose what's really
interesting about this.

01:12:51.210 --> 01:12:52.760
And I guess this sort of goes back

01:12:52.760 --> 01:12:55.120
to this question of co-presence
and historicity as well.

01:12:55.120 --> 01:12:57.050
Is that what...

01:12:57.050 --> 01:13:01.570
I think what people are
invoking here is not so much

01:13:05.140 --> 01:13:08.340
a sort of absolutely good British past

01:13:08.340 --> 01:13:12.720
but a kind of ideal relationship
with a benevolent state

01:13:12.720 --> 01:13:15.990
which they've kind of
come to see as a baseline.

01:13:15.990 --> 01:13:17.824
So as far as they're concerned,

01:13:17.824 --> 01:13:20.450
the British government, as they say,

01:13:20.450 --> 01:13:22.030
kind of laid...

01:13:22.030 --> 01:13:24.590
It set the standards for
what their relationship

01:13:24.590 --> 01:13:25.960
with the state should be like.

01:13:25.960 --> 01:13:27.320
The state should take care of them

01:13:27.320 --> 01:13:29.330
but it should not eat their lands.

01:13:29.330 --> 01:13:31.230
And what they want from
the Malaysian government

01:13:31.230 --> 01:13:33.080
is a return to that relationship.

01:13:33.080 --> 01:13:36.610
Give us development but
don't take our lands.

01:13:36.610 --> 01:13:40.060
And there's an interesting
sort of constant hopefulness

01:13:40.060 --> 01:13:42.980
about the way they sort
of engage with politicians

01:13:42.980 --> 01:13:44.660
and with development
projects that they think,

01:13:44.660 --> 01:13:45.849
well, maybe this will work,

01:13:45.849 --> 01:13:49.360
maybe this time this
project might actually

01:13:49.360 --> 01:13:50.840
give us development and work

01:13:50.840 --> 01:13:52.610
and still allow us to keep our land.

01:13:52.610 --> 01:13:55.950
So there's a very interesting
kind of eternal optimism

01:13:55.950 --> 01:13:58.763
at play here that comes with
that nostalgia, I think.

01:13:59.630 --> 01:14:00.480
And so I guess they've got

01:14:00.480 --> 01:14:02.380
their own sort of projections, right?

01:14:02.380 --> 01:14:04.530
They've got their own sort of
future speculative projects

01:14:04.530 --> 01:14:06.560
about how at some point they will return

01:14:06.560 --> 01:14:08.390
to this kind of ideal governments,

01:14:08.390 --> 01:14:11.343
this ideal Bidayuh state relationship.

01:14:12.680 --> 01:14:14.880
And I think that is
particularly interesting

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:18.310
because that is them trying
to project themselves

01:14:19.580 --> 01:14:21.660
into what I described earlier

01:14:21.660 --> 01:14:23.833
as a very powerful homogenizing force.

01:14:24.710 --> 01:14:26.960
You know, it's them basically saying,

01:14:26.960 --> 01:14:29.960
well, we don't wanna
be completely eaten up

01:14:29.960 --> 01:14:30.870
by the Malaysian state

01:14:30.870 --> 01:14:32.670
but we really do wanna be Malaysian.

01:14:32.670 --> 01:14:35.440
We really do believe in
being Malaysian citizens

01:14:35.440 --> 01:14:37.790
and we want our rights,
we want the development

01:14:37.790 --> 01:14:40.040
and the other good things that would do

01:14:40.040 --> 01:14:41.423
so please don't forget us.

01:14:42.580 --> 01:14:44.010
And that is a form of co-presence

01:14:44.010 --> 01:14:45.050
that they're constantly asserting

01:14:45.050 --> 01:14:47.110
but also a form of sameness.

01:14:47.110 --> 01:14:49.370
Which I find a really interesting kind of

01:14:50.940 --> 01:14:53.030
idea to deal with, I guess

01:14:53.030 --> 01:14:54.800
partly because it sort of,

01:14:54.800 --> 01:14:56.657
it probably sort of pushes up against

01:14:56.657 --> 01:14:59.490
my own kind of anthropological instincts,

01:14:59.490 --> 01:15:01.473
which is why do you wanna do this?

01:15:02.480 --> 01:15:04.730
But I guess it's pragmatic.

01:15:04.730 --> 01:15:05.840
They wanna be Malaysians.

01:15:05.840 --> 01:15:07.390
They wanna be part of the state

01:15:08.780 --> 01:15:12.160
that it's sort of my job to
try and understand how and why.

01:15:14.210 --> 01:15:15.670
- Thank you, Liana.

01:15:15.670 --> 01:15:17.500
Liana, we're getting close to 1:20.

01:15:17.500 --> 01:15:19.730
I have time, but I am mindful of...

01:15:19.730 --> 01:15:22.260
I didn't tell you how
long we would keep you.

01:15:22.260 --> 01:15:23.500
And if you have the energy

01:15:23.500 --> 01:15:25.520
you can answer as long as you wish

01:15:25.520 --> 01:15:29.220
but we have two more questions
to that I'd like to hear.

01:15:29.220 --> 01:15:31.330
Would you like to hear both
questions at the same time?

01:15:31.330 --> 01:15:33.110
Or should I go one at a time?

01:15:33.110 --> 01:15:34.000
- I don't mind.

01:15:34.000 --> 01:15:34.833
- It's okay.

01:15:34.833 --> 01:15:37.970
So we'll go with Kristin S first.

01:15:37.970 --> 01:15:40.113
And Kristin if you can introduce yourself.

01:15:41.730 --> 01:15:43.680
- [Kristin] Hello, thank you very much.

01:15:48.320 --> 01:15:53.320
I wanted to ask you about
to reflect a little bit,

01:15:53.510 --> 01:15:56.600
if possible what scene...

01:15:56.600 --> 01:16:00.380
We seem to be so comfortable glossing over

01:16:01.670 --> 01:16:06.480
the influence of Christianity

01:16:06.480 --> 01:16:10.520
and this homogenizing
force that you speak of

01:16:11.700 --> 01:16:15.730
and even in the way in
which it contributes

01:16:15.730 --> 01:16:18.610
to this shaping of what you...

01:16:18.610 --> 01:16:21.883
Of what the relationship
with the state should be,

01:16:23.626 --> 01:16:28.137
and that in all of our understanding

01:16:31.690 --> 01:16:36.690
currently suggests that the
great importance of biodiversity

01:16:41.680 --> 01:16:43.940
and how do we...

01:16:43.940 --> 01:16:47.670
It's so troubling to me to hear the way

01:16:47.670 --> 01:16:50.310
in which you describe young people

01:16:50.310 --> 01:16:53.220
not wanting to have even to be...

01:16:53.220 --> 01:16:56.720
Have any understanding of their origin,

01:16:56.720 --> 01:16:59.740
of their cultural identity

01:16:59.740 --> 01:17:03.437
because they've been so
brainwashed to believe

01:17:07.000 --> 01:17:10.200
that somehow this is going
to be detrimental to them.

01:17:10.200 --> 01:17:15.200
And why is that we have this
notion that somehow crucifixes

01:17:17.110 --> 01:17:21.603
and are valid tools for
protection and that,

01:17:23.050 --> 01:17:27.780
and how is it that we become as academics

01:17:27.780 --> 01:17:32.780
often so comfortable with
this dispassionate observation

01:17:34.160 --> 01:17:39.160
of the loss of a language of
a whole body of knowledge,

01:17:40.650 --> 01:17:43.590
this hierarchical valuing of

01:17:43.590 --> 01:17:48.010
what is the gift of understanding

01:17:48.010 --> 01:17:49.830
that we're bringing in education

01:17:49.830 --> 01:17:51.780
and the arrogance that we show

01:17:51.780 --> 01:17:56.780
in not how do we overcome this sense

01:17:59.830 --> 01:18:04.830
of trying to actually learn from cultures

01:18:06.760 --> 01:18:10.260
that have been in existence
for thousands of years

01:18:10.260 --> 01:18:13.950
very successfully and
actually expand our knowledge

01:18:13.950 --> 01:18:17.383
as opposed to contracting
it and making it smaller?

01:18:18.780 --> 01:18:19.613
- Sure. Yeah.

01:18:19.613 --> 01:18:20.480
No, thank you.

01:18:20.480 --> 01:18:22.470
I mean, I have to say this is something

01:18:22.470 --> 01:18:24.830
that troubles me a lot as well.

01:18:24.830 --> 01:18:27.610
And what I...

01:18:27.610 --> 01:18:31.530
Earlier on, I kind of said,
I went into this research

01:18:31.530 --> 01:18:34.750
very, very hostile towards
missionaries and Christianity

01:18:34.750 --> 01:18:38.150
precisely because of this problem.

01:18:38.150 --> 01:18:43.150
But I think this is where
we do have one of those

01:18:43.250 --> 01:18:46.280
sort of classic ethical
tensions in anthropology,

01:18:46.280 --> 01:18:49.580
which is actually very,
very difficult to get round.

01:18:49.580 --> 01:18:50.707
And this is this...

01:18:50.707 --> 01:18:53.870
And that is this question of how...

01:18:53.870 --> 01:18:57.130
Of what you do if the people
that you're working with

01:18:57.130 --> 01:19:01.870
fervently and wholeheartedly
subscribe to something

01:19:01.870 --> 01:19:03.830
and believe in something
and craft their lives

01:19:03.830 --> 01:19:07.380
around something that you
personally might find repugnant

01:19:07.380 --> 01:19:09.180
or find extremely problematic.

01:19:09.180 --> 01:19:12.960
And I do find Christianity
extremely problematic

01:19:12.960 --> 01:19:14.053
in all sorts of ways,

01:19:14.980 --> 01:19:19.980
but I also acknowledge
that if I were to approach

01:19:20.010 --> 01:19:25.010
the field work that I did
from that starting point,

01:19:25.270 --> 01:19:29.260
I wouldn't have been able to
do field work in this village.

01:19:29.260 --> 01:19:32.840
I would not have been able
to understand very much

01:19:32.840 --> 01:19:35.003
about my interlocutors lives.

01:19:36.160 --> 01:19:39.430
And so I guess, yes, I mean, in this case

01:19:39.430 --> 01:19:44.430
I did make a choice to suspend
my sort of personal concerns

01:19:45.010 --> 01:19:47.620
about the influence and the
very detrimental influence

01:19:47.620 --> 01:19:51.770
that Christianity has had
in all sorts of societies.

01:19:51.770 --> 01:19:55.900
And to actually try to
understand what it was

01:19:55.900 --> 01:19:59.120
about Christianity that was
so powerful in people's lives,

01:19:59.120 --> 01:20:02.343
how it was changing their lives.

01:20:03.520 --> 01:20:07.210
And that was basically
something I had to do

01:20:07.210 --> 01:20:11.930
in order to understand the
people that I was living with.

01:20:11.930 --> 01:20:15.060
I guess the other thing
to sort of say is that

01:20:16.670 --> 01:20:19.440
in many ways, the young
people have embraced

01:20:20.760 --> 01:20:22.660
aspects of their past.

01:20:22.660 --> 01:20:24.680
The Bidayuh language is not dying.

01:20:24.680 --> 01:20:27.410
People are still actively
speaking in the villages,

01:20:27.410 --> 01:20:28.540
it's busy...

01:20:28.540 --> 01:20:33.010
People are basically recreating
it and playing around

01:20:33.010 --> 01:20:34.520
with it on social media.

01:20:34.520 --> 01:20:35.660
It has been absolutely fascinating

01:20:35.660 --> 01:20:37.880
kind of watching new kinds of terminology,

01:20:37.880 --> 01:20:41.423
new spellings emerge on
Facebook with my friends,

01:20:42.350 --> 01:20:43.560
where they really are kind of...

01:20:43.560 --> 01:20:46.470
It is still very much a living language.

01:20:46.470 --> 01:20:48.060
And there are also many other aspects

01:20:48.060 --> 01:20:52.335
of kind of earlier bits of non-animus life

01:20:52.335 --> 01:20:55.490
that have not completely been squashed.

01:20:55.490 --> 01:20:59.320
So I think in that sense,
what they were very concerned

01:20:59.320 --> 01:21:03.510
about was a very specific
aspect of the old ways

01:21:03.510 --> 01:21:07.380
which was precisely how you
relate to the old spirits

01:21:07.380 --> 01:21:10.630
and how that might actually
cause trouble for them

01:21:10.630 --> 01:21:13.150
how it might actually put
them in personal danger.

01:21:13.150 --> 01:21:15.640
And this was a very deeply felt concern.

01:21:15.640 --> 01:21:17.430
It wasn't just, I don't
wanna know about my,

01:21:17.430 --> 01:21:19.430
the old ways because I'm Christian.

01:21:19.430 --> 01:21:23.330
No, it was kind of like, if I
inadvertently screw up on this

01:21:23.330 --> 01:21:24.573
my child could die.

01:21:25.820 --> 01:21:28.330
And it was, I didn't really
feel that it was my place

01:21:28.330 --> 01:21:29.480
to then come in there and say,

01:21:29.480 --> 01:21:30.950
well, actually you're doing things wrong

01:21:30.950 --> 01:21:34.610
because you are disrespecting your old,

01:21:34.610 --> 01:21:36.080
the the old ways, your old culture.

01:21:36.080 --> 01:21:39.220
I mean, I certainly felt
it, but it was not...

01:21:39.220 --> 01:21:42.100
I felt my place to pass
that sort of judgment

01:21:42.100 --> 01:21:43.450
during my field work.

01:21:43.450 --> 01:21:46.370
And so I did maintain a
certain sort of agnosticism

01:21:46.370 --> 01:21:50.023
about what I was studying
and trying to understand.

01:21:51.670 --> 01:21:54.380
And I mean, it was also very interesting

01:21:54.380 --> 01:21:56.750
that the elderly
practitioners were very...

01:21:56.750 --> 01:21:58.140
They were quite sort of blaise about it,

01:21:58.140 --> 01:21:59.437
which I also found interesting.

01:21:59.437 --> 01:22:02.980
And they said, well, life ways
are changing all the times,

01:22:02.980 --> 01:22:04.200
rituals change,

01:22:04.200 --> 01:22:06.410
the old spirits are gonna
become Christian eventually

01:22:06.410 --> 01:22:08.970
or they might just go off
to their homes in the hills

01:22:08.970 --> 01:22:12.563
and the rivers, because
we're always changing.

01:22:13.600 --> 01:22:18.240
So I guess it was that aspect
of Christian conversion

01:22:18.240 --> 01:22:19.460
that I was trying to understand

01:22:19.460 --> 01:22:21.360
and how actually the sort of Christianity,

01:22:21.360 --> 01:22:24.990
this sort of Christian life
did come to suffuse relations

01:22:24.990 --> 01:22:26.670
in the village.

01:22:26.670 --> 01:22:30.420
Yeah, and it felt
uncomfortable at many points,

01:22:30.420 --> 01:22:35.410
but I guess in terms of
trying to actually empathize

01:22:35.410 --> 01:22:40.410
and understand my interlocutors lives

01:22:40.500 --> 01:22:43.210
that was kind of suspension

01:22:43.210 --> 01:22:45.313
of my own doubt that I had to take.

01:22:49.320 --> 01:22:50.360
- Thank you, Liana.

01:22:50.360 --> 01:22:52.440
It reminded me of something
you said earlier too

01:22:52.440 --> 01:22:54.770
about when you were talking about ontology

01:22:54.770 --> 01:22:59.190
about the need to have a kind
of humility as an anthropology

01:22:59.190 --> 01:23:02.770
or a humble anthropology that
you had talked about before.

01:23:02.770 --> 01:23:05.780
We'll take the last
question from David Lindsay.

01:23:05.780 --> 01:23:06.613
- Thank you.

01:23:06.613 --> 01:23:09.830
Thank you for this talk
has been very interesting.

01:23:09.830 --> 01:23:12.570
Regarding the people you have focused on,

01:23:12.570 --> 01:23:16.870
are the older ways in general
more environmentally conscious

01:23:16.870 --> 01:23:19.160
than the new Christian views?

01:23:19.160 --> 01:23:21.370
Do these people become less

01:23:21.370 --> 01:23:23.560
or more environmentally conscious

01:23:23.560 --> 01:23:25.173
with embracing Christianity?

01:23:28.920 --> 01:23:30.793
- Yeah, that's a tough one to answer.

01:23:32.150 --> 01:23:35.410
It's a tough one to answer
because the sort of big push

01:23:35.410 --> 01:23:38.050
to convert to Christianity
went hand in hand

01:23:38.050 --> 01:23:39.933
with a move away from rice planting.

01:23:43.369 --> 01:23:47.630
And so there were, for
example and there still are

01:23:47.630 --> 01:23:51.490
various kind of traditional
ways of, I guess

01:23:51.490 --> 01:23:54.110
what we would now call them
sustainable farming, right?

01:23:54.110 --> 01:23:55.320
Which people still observe.

01:23:55.320 --> 01:23:57.330
So you know, for example, there was a...

01:23:57.330 --> 01:23:59.120
There are certain kind of areas of land

01:23:59.120 --> 01:24:01.420
that are set aside for
use by the community.

01:24:01.420 --> 01:24:02.880
You know not a single household

01:24:02.880 --> 01:24:06.040
which is the usual unit of
ownership but a community

01:24:06.040 --> 01:24:08.700
and people are allowed
to sort of take turns

01:24:08.700 --> 01:24:10.060
cultivating that piece of land.

01:24:10.060 --> 01:24:12.420
But then the sort of general
understanding is that

01:24:12.420 --> 01:24:13.330
if the (speaks in foreign language)

01:24:13.330 --> 01:24:15.480
which is like the spirit of the place

01:24:15.480 --> 01:24:16.570
finds that there's one family

01:24:16.570 --> 01:24:18.790
that's been over cultivating
it or doing too much

01:24:18.790 --> 01:24:20.680
then it gets really cross
and then your crop fails.

01:24:20.680 --> 01:24:22.850
So just don't do that.

01:24:22.850 --> 01:24:24.930
And people are actually
quite good at following that.

01:24:24.930 --> 01:24:26.356
They may or may not say it's to do with

01:24:26.356 --> 01:24:27.770
(speaks in foreign language) now,

01:24:27.770 --> 01:24:28.900
even though they...

01:24:28.900 --> 01:24:30.190
Generally, there's an understanding

01:24:30.190 --> 01:24:32.460
that the (speaks in foreign
language) still exists

01:24:32.460 --> 01:24:36.079
but they're generally quite
good even including today

01:24:36.079 --> 01:24:39.640
in Christian villages
at following that rule.

01:24:39.640 --> 01:24:42.180
So you could say in some ways
that actually it's not really

01:24:42.180 --> 01:24:43.990
made a huge difference.

01:24:43.990 --> 01:24:46.090
I think the sort of bigger change has been

01:24:47.190 --> 01:24:48.720
just with sort of development

01:24:48.720 --> 01:24:52.070
and the extension of
infrastructure to these villages.

01:24:52.070 --> 01:24:54.420
So the village where I did my PhD

01:24:54.420 --> 01:24:57.410
had a road built, a
TOD road extended to it

01:24:57.410 --> 01:25:00.310
in the sort of 19, late 1990s.

01:25:00.310 --> 01:25:01.400
So it was...

01:25:01.400 --> 01:25:04.020
It had been a very sort of
Stony gravel road before that.

01:25:04.020 --> 01:25:05.490
And it was TOD

01:25:05.490 --> 01:25:08.210
and that caused a huge demographic shift

01:25:08.210 --> 01:25:13.210
in people effectively
becoming village commuters,

01:25:13.450 --> 01:25:16.920
they go to school and work
in urban areas, earn wages.

01:25:16.920 --> 01:25:19.120
They came back, they lived in the village,

01:25:19.120 --> 01:25:20.213
they'd be villages.

01:25:21.850 --> 01:25:23.290
And so that actually meant that they...

01:25:23.290 --> 01:25:24.950
Most of them were not relying on

01:25:26.340 --> 01:25:27.770
subsistence farming anymore.

01:25:27.770 --> 01:25:28.603
So it was...

01:25:28.603 --> 01:25:30.090
You'd still have...

01:25:30.090 --> 01:25:32.070
People would still cultivate their land

01:25:32.070 --> 01:25:33.970
because they were afraid of land grabs.

01:25:33.970 --> 01:25:36.100
So there was a kind of political point.

01:25:36.100 --> 01:25:37.370
They didn't want the state to come in

01:25:37.370 --> 01:25:41.220
and see these seemingly unused
or abandoned pieces of land

01:25:41.220 --> 01:25:43.350
and say, oh, we're gonna take those.

01:25:43.350 --> 01:25:44.510
It was very much a case of,

01:25:44.510 --> 01:25:45.860
no, we're gonna keep our lands going

01:25:45.860 --> 01:25:48.310
even if we're never gonna
feed ourselves from it.

01:25:49.250 --> 01:25:51.360
So, I mean, it's a tricky one

01:25:51.360 --> 01:25:53.960
because you can't really separate

01:25:53.960 --> 01:25:55.200
the conversion to Christianity

01:25:55.200 --> 01:25:57.283
from the urbanization process.

01:25:58.670 --> 01:26:01.630
In a way there's just been much less done

01:26:01.630 --> 01:26:03.030
to the local environment

01:26:04.450 --> 01:26:06.610
since people are started
converting to Christianity

01:26:06.610 --> 01:26:07.443
but that's nothing...

01:26:07.443 --> 01:26:08.990
That's got nothing to
do with Christianity.

01:26:08.990 --> 01:26:11.433
It's just got to do with urbanization.

01:26:12.550 --> 01:26:13.850
- [David] Yeah. Thank you.

01:26:15.290 --> 01:26:16.560
- Well, thank you, Liana.

01:26:16.560 --> 01:26:18.963
This has been a fascinating conversation.

01:26:18.963 --> 01:26:21.980
I really appreciate you taking
so much time with us today

01:26:21.980 --> 01:26:25.700
to share so much about your
long-term ongoing research

01:26:25.700 --> 01:26:26.543
in Borneo.

01:26:27.620 --> 01:26:30.020
I'm gonna bring the session to a close.

01:26:30.020 --> 01:26:32.212
There is a question for you in the chat

01:26:32.212 --> 01:26:35.740
that if you wanna respond
you can send me the answer

01:26:35.740 --> 01:26:38.540
and I'll send it to the quest audience,

01:26:38.540 --> 01:26:40.690
but I think it is now 1:30

01:26:40.690 --> 01:26:42.250
so we should bring it to a close.

01:26:42.250 --> 01:26:45.380
So everyone join me in thanking Liana Chua

01:26:45.380 --> 01:26:46.920
for such a wonderful talk.

01:26:46.920 --> 01:26:48.670
And we'll see you in two weeks

01:26:48.670 --> 01:26:51.110
for a talk with James
